\noindent\textbf{Discount Factor Search.} When searching the discount factor $d_i$ for a solution $\bm{x}_i$ assigned to cell $e$, our goal is to ensure that inserting $d_i{\bf f}(\bm{x}_i)$ to $T_e$ closes its gap to $F_e$ by a fraction. To quantify this goal, we use the hypervolume improvement of the discounted objectives over the pre-insertion threshold front, i.e., $\mbox{HVI}^{disc}(d_i) = \mbox{HVI}_r(d_i{\bf f}(\bm{x}_i), T_e)$, as a heuristic for the gap between $T_e$ and $F_e$ closed by inserting $d_i{\bf f}(\bm{x}_i)$. We observe that $\mbox{HVI}^{disc}(d_i)$ is a strictly monotonic function over $d_i \in [0,1]$, and is upper-bounded by the hypervolume improvement of the raw, undiscounted objectives $\mbox{HVI}^{disc}(1) = \mbox{HVI}_r({\bf f}(\bm{x}_i), T_e)$. This allows us to perform bisection search over $d_i \in [0,1]$, terminating when $\mbox{HVI}^{disc}(d_i) \approx \alpha\mbox{HVI}^{disc}(1) = \alpha\mbox{HVI}_r({\bf f}(\bm{x}_i), T_e)$. In other words, we find a $d_i$ that closes a fraction $\alpha$ of the maximum closable gap. We refer to the fraction hyperparameter $\alpha \in (0,1)$ as MO-CMA-MAE's \textit{learning rate}, because it can be interpreted as the learning rate of each cell's hypervolume. Figure~\ref{fig:bisection} illustrates an example run of this bisection search, in which the target HVI (yellow) is $\alpha = 0.2$ of the raw HVI (red), and the search pointer (green) approximates the target after a few iterations. We provide further details on bisection search implementation in Appendix~\ref{sec:bisec}.



% The choice of discount factor $d_i$ plays a key role in our $T_e$ implementation, since it controls the gap between $T_e$ and $F_e$. If $d_i$ is too small, then the discounted objective value $d_i{\bf f}(\bm{x})$ may become dominated, and its insertion into $T_e$ may fail. On the other hand, if $d_i$ is too large, then $T_e$ coincides with $F_e$ instead of lagging behind. Ideally, we should choose $d_i$ such that $d_i{\bf f}(\bm{x}) \not\preceq T_e \iff {\bf f}(\bm{x}) \not\preceq T_e$ in order to ensure that every non-dominated solution is inserted. Moreover, the ideal $d_i$ should make $T_e$ gradually approximate $F_e$ as cell $e$ is visited more times, boosting HVI improvement scores for initial explorations on $e$, but eventually tuning down the boost to allow the optimization to converge. We note that we can control the hypervolume improvement of a candidate using $d_i$, with $\mbox{HVI}_{discount} = \mbox{HVI}_r(d_i{\bf f}(\bm{x}), T_e)$, and both requirements can be satisfied by fulfilling some condition on $\mbox{HVI}_{discount}$. For the first requirement, we can enforce $\mbox{HVI}_{discount} > 0 \iff \mbox{HVI}_r({\bf f}(\bm{x}), T_e) > 0$, which is equivalent because $\mbox{HVI}_r({\bf f}(\bm{x}), T_e) > 0 \iff {\bf f}(\bm{x}) \not\preceq T_e$. For the second requirement, we observe that, assuming ${\bf f}(\bm{x}) \not\preceq F_e$, $\mbox{HVI}_r({\bf f}(\bm{x}), T_e)$ quantifies the local gap between $T_e$ and $F_e$ at ${\bf f}(\bm{x})$. If we enforce $\mbox{HVI}_{discount} = \mbox{HVI}_r({\bf f}(\bm{x}), T_e)/\alpha$ such that $\alpha$ is constant across all insertions, we close a fraction of the local gap between $T_e$ and $F_e$ with every insertion, and thereby anneal the gap closure. Given these two observations, we propose to search for a $d_{target}$ such that $\mbox{HVI}_{discount} = \mbox{HVI}_r(d_{target}{\bf f}(\bm{x}), T_e) = \alpha \mbox{HVI}_r({\bf f}(\bm{x}), T_e)$, where $\alpha \in [0,1]$ is a hyperparameter that controls how quickly we want to close the gap between $T_e$ and $F_e$. We refer to $\alpha$ as \textit{learning rate}, since it can be interpreted as a learning rate for the hypervolume. In this work, we perform the search on $d_i \approx d_{target}$ using bisection, and return an approximate $d_i$ when $0 < \alpha \mbox{HVI}_r({\bf f}(\bm{x}), T_e) - \mbox{HVI}_r(d_i{\bf f}(\bm{x}), T_e) < \epsilon$, where $\epsilon$ is an error tolerance hyperparameter. We highlight that we restrict the error to be in the negative direction, i.e $\mbox{HVI}_r(d_i{\bf f}(\bm{x}), T_e) < \alpha \mbox{HVI}_r({\bf f}(\bm{x}), T_e)$. This is because $T_e$ prunes dominated solutions after every insertion, and inserting a large $d_i{\bf f}(\bm{x})$ will remove all ${\bf f} \in T_e \prec d_i{\bf f}(\bm{x})$. In other words, the improvement of $T_e$ is non-reversible, and a $d_i - d_{target}$ error in the positive direction will skip over some objective-space regions that haven't actually been covered. Finally, we note that this discount factor search is performed for every ${\bf f}(\bm{x})$ to be inserted into $T_e$. Although this is a costly operation, this guarantees every insertion closes an approximate $\alpha$-fraction of the local gap, and gives us fine control over the annealing behavior. We show an illustration for the discount factor bisection search in Figure~\ref{fig:bisection}.


