\section{Experiments}


\subsection{Datasets}
% 为验证算法在大量数据集上的普适性，参考【\cite{da2020distributed}】中数据集的选取，我们的实验混合使用了其中15个包含不同特征的现实世界和人工基准数据集（real world and artificial benchmark datasets），这些数据集可以在【\cite{Bache+Lichman:2013,ClusteringDatasets,dataset}】中获取，并在表ref{tab:datasets}中总结了他们的特征。实验开始前，对所有数据集采取Min-Max归一化，将其特征缩放到范围[0, 1]，并经过补码编码得到输入向量。
To evaluate the applicability of our algorithm in diverse datasets, we adopted the dataset selection strategy proposed in \cite{da2020distributed}. Our experiments involve a combination of 15 real world and artificial benchmark datasets with various characteristics, which are accessible from \cite{Bache+Lichman:2013,ClusteringDatasets,ilc2011gravitational}. The key properties of these datasets are summarized in Table~\ref{tab:datasets}. Before the experiments, all datasets were preprocessed using Min-Max normalization to scale their characteristic values to the range \([0, 1]\) and further transformed into input vectors through complement coding \cite{da2020distributed}.




\subsection{Experimental Setup}
% 我们的实验出发点是模拟一个缺乏先验知识的用户在使用基于ART的聚类方法（ART-based clustering methods）时得到的聚类表现。因此在聚类算法方面，我们选取了FuzzyART，CM-ART、AM-ART、HI-ART和SA-ART五种方法作为比较，因为它们预设参数数量少，同时都具有一定的对ρ的自适应能力。
% 在参数设置方面，我们没有对每个数据集进行复杂的最佳参数的选取，而是在所有数据集上，每个方法采用统一的比较合理、常用的参数设置不改变，以确保对先验知识的低依赖性，并保持公平的比较。具体来讲，对于choice parameter α统一设为10-3，学习率β采用0.5表示中等的学习程度；SA-ART所需的λ设为0.5，表示频率和稳定性一样重要；对于AM-ART中的σ，我们在10-1、10-2、10-3、10-4、10-5中选取10-4，因为这个值与其他值相比，能在大部分数据集上获得合理的类簇数目。同样，我们将SA-ART中的δ和CM-ART中的Δ也设为10-4；除CM-ART和HI-ART以外，其他方法都会迭代多轮，并且t_max为50，终止条件与伪代码第23行相同。CM-ART和HI-ART则只执行一轮，因为在实验中发现它们产生的类簇数目随着迭代迅速增加，我们推测是CMR\cite{meng2015adaptive}引起ρ的大幅度调整并不适应我们所选择的数据集。对于所有方法的初始警戒参数rho，我们让它成为实验中唯一被不断调整的变量，在[0.05,0.95]中扫描，步长为0.01，在数据集的10次随机顺序下执行，记录每个rho对应的指标的平均值。
% 本实验采用常用的外部聚类有效性指数（external cluster validity index）NMI和ARI来评估经过聚类得到的结果的质量，它们的值越高往往代表越好的聚类表现。归一化互信息（NMI）量化了预测标签与ground truth标签之间的互信息，并将结果缩放到 \（[0, 1]\）范围内。调整后的兰德指数（ARI）由兰德指数（\text{RI}）衍生而来，它将聚类结果视为一系列配对决策，并根据决策的正确率对其进行评估，范围是[-1,1]。更多细节可见[\cite{zhou2024comprehensive}]。

Our experiments aim to simulate the clustering performance of a user lacking prior knowledge when employing ART-based clustering methods. We compared our method with Fuzzy ART\cite{carpenter1991fuzzy}, and four methods mentioned in Introduction: CM-ART\cite{meng2013vigilance}, AM-ART\cite{meng2013vigilance}, HI-ART\cite{meng2015adaptive}, and SA-ART\cite{meng2019salience}. These methods were selected for their minimal parameter requirements and adaptive mechanisms for the vigilance parameter.

For parameter settings, we avoided complex optimization for individual datasets. Instead, we applied a uniform set of reasonable and commonly used parameters across all datasets to minimize dependence on prior knowledge and ensure a fair comparison. Specifically, the choice parameter \(\alpha\) was set to \(10^{-3}\). The learning rate $\beta$ was set to 0.5, reflecting a moderate level of learning. In SA-ART, \(\lambda = 0.5\) balanced frequency and stability. For AM-ART, we evaluated $\sigma$ from the set $\{10^{-1}, 10^{-2}, 10^{-3}, 10^{-4}, 10^{-5}\}$ and selected $10^{-4}$, as it yielded reasonable cluster numbers in most datasets. Similarly, we set $\delta$ in SA-ART and $\Delta$ in CM-ART to $10^{-4}$. Other methods were iterated with $t_{\text{max}} = 50$, following the termination criteria specified in line 23 of the pseudocode, except for CM-ART and HI-ART, which were executed only once due to rapid cluster growth with further iterations. The initial vigilance parameter $\rho$ was the sole variable, scanned in the range $[0.05, 0.95]$ with a step size of 0.01. Each setting was run 10 times on randomly ordered datasets, and the average performance metrics corresponding to each \(\rho\) were recorded.

We employ two commonly used external cluster validity indices, Normalized Mutual Information (NMI) and Adjusted Rand Index (ARI), to evaluate the quality of the clustering results. Higher values of these indices generally indicate better clustering performance. 
% NMI quantifies the mutual information between the predicted labels and the ground truth labels, scaling the result into the range \([0, 1]\). ARI is derived from the Rand Index (RI), which treats the clustering result as a series of pairwise decisions and evaluates it based on the rate of correct decisions. The range of ARI is \([-1, 1]\).
More details can be found in \cite{zhou2024comprehensive}.

