\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
	First, we determine a set $V$ of Pareto optimal vectors with cardinality at least $|F^*|/2 \geq |I|/2^{d/2+1}$ and show that it will be covered in $O(n)$ generations with probability at least $1-e^{-\Omega(n)}$. Let $\beta:=\sqrt[d/2]{2} \in \Theta(1)$ and let $V:=\Bigl\{v \in (\mathbb{N}_0)^d \mid v_i \in \Bigl[\frac{\frac{2n(\beta-1)}{d}+2k-3}{2\beta}-1+k,\frac{\frac{2n(\beta+1)}{d}-2k+3}{2\beta}+1+k \Bigl] \text{ for all }i \in [d] \Bigl\}$. %For $n$ sufficiently large (for example for $n \geq \frac{m}{1-1/\beta}$) 
