\section{Introduction}

\textbf{Background.}
%
Nowadays, with the booming of internet technology and digital content growth, the information explosion is becoming a more and more serious problem affecting user experience.
%
To help users find the most relevant information, the RecSys became more and more important to many platforms, such as Kuaishou (short-video and live-streaming), Taobao (shopping)  and so on.
%
To our knowledge, in industry, these platform RecSys are mostly based on collaborative filtering idea(e.g.,YouTube~\cite{youtube}), which learns user preferences through multiple-binary classification tasks of massive user behaviors, (e.g., is click/dislike/long-view/buy the item?).
%
In general, CF is extremely simple, efficient, and accurate in capturing users’ positive behavior signals.
%
However, it primarily focuses on probability calculations rather than truly understanding the meaning behind the items, making it difficult to interpret negative behaviors (e.g., dislikes).
%
Particularly, instead of implicit binary feedback (e.g., clicks, dislikes, long views), users also leave some explicit feedback (e.g., comments, reviews) to describes his/her feelings about this interaction further, which leave space to understand why a user likes or dislikes certain items.
%
Hence, a hot topic is to utilize the content-based reviews to provide semantic knowledge to enhance RecSys.

\textbf{Related work.} 
%
In this research direction, a major task is Rating prediction, which is formulated as follows: given a user’s historical reviews and item reviews from other users, predict the rating for a user-item pair.
%
Early works such as DeepCoNN~\cite{DeepCoNN} and D-attn~\cite{dattn} follow a disentangled two-tower paradigm. 
%
They generate user and item representations separately and compute the final score via dot product (DeepCoNN and D-attn employ CNNs and Transformers~\cite{attention} as their towers, respectively).
%
In this paradigm, ANR~\cite{anr} extends D-attn by generating multiple representations for users/items and using user/item-aware weights to fuse them.
%
The above methods rely on small models trained from scratch. However, with the rise of large language models (LLMs), recent works tend to leverage pre-trained LLMs to enhance model capabilities, as semantic knowledge provides generalized information.
%
For example, LLM-based approaches like RGCL~\cite{RGCL} use BERT~\cite{bert} to encode reviews, while LoID~\cite{xu2024enhancing} employs LoRA~\cite{LoRA} to fine-tune the LLM, delivering more powerful semantic representations.
%

% 

\textbf{Motivation.}
Although these methods raised model ability with content information, they ignore the following problems: 
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*,align=left]
    %
    \item \textit{Unexplainable Semantic Compression}:
    Current LLM-based RecSys suffer from opaque decision pathways: they compress text semantics into unexplainable latent representations, obscuring the logical connections in the prediction process.
    %
    % 
    \item \textit{Highly Computation Cost}:
    %
    Previous works always leverage large language models (LLMs) in an end-to-end manner incur exorbitant computational demands, primarily due to the immense scale of billion-parameter models(e.g., GPT~\cite{gpt-4}), 
    % 
    leading to prohibitively long training times and high inference latency, making them impractical for many real-world scenarios\footnote{Training LoID for one epoch on 100M dataset requires 24h with a NVIDIA T4 GPU.}. 
    %
\end{itemize}
\textbf{Our Work.}
In this paper, we present RecCoT, a novel recommendation framework featuring two key technical contributions:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*,align=left]
\item \textit{Slow Interpretable CoT Generation via Larger LLM}:
Inspired by the great success of reasoning ability in NLP, we consider to utilize such training paradigm to enhance recommendation, e.g., GRPO~\cite{deepseekmath}.
%
However, in RecSys, there exists a serious long-tail problem (e.g, rating=5 data are more than 90\% in many datasets), thus naive GRPO training strategy suffers from reward hacking~\cite{think} problem.
%
To this end, we first propose a frequency-aware reward policy to supervise a larger LLM to adapt the long-tail rating distribution.
%
It introduces an accuracy reward mechanism to ensure explainable reasoning generation.
% 
\item \textit{Fast Semantic Embedding Alignment via Smaller LLM}:
%
%
Since the reasoning model always require billion-scale model parameter, thus the end-to-end training paradigm is impossible for the downstreaming RecSys.
%
Therefore, in this paper, we apply a cache paradigm to provide the semantic knowledge for the downstreaming RecSys to avoid the massive computation.
%
To ensure the generated semantic embedding could align with the downstreaming task, we further fine-tune a smaller LLM to generate the semantic embeddings for each reviews.
%
Thanks for our Slow-Fast LLM designing, our RecSys model can be greatly streamlined to achieve lightweight and efficient prediction.
% 
\end{enumerate}
\textbf{Contributions.} 
Our contributions are summarized as follows:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*,align=left]
%
\item We propose a decoupled reasoning-execution framework: Larger LLMs handle interpretable CoT generation while smaller LLMs perform high-speed semantic alignment. 
%
\item We devise a reward strategy to adapt long-tail distribution that ensures the quality of CoT. 
\item We conduct detailed analyses with SOTA methods.

\end{itemize}

%
\section{Preliminary}
\subsection{Problem Statement}
%
In this work, we consider a rating prediction task as the primary research goal.
%
Specifically, given \textbf{observed} Review-Rating ($t_{u,i}$, $r_{u,i}$) of user-item pair ($u$,$i$) as corpus to train a model, our goal is to predict other \textbf{un-observed} user-item rating score $r_{\mathrm{u,i}}$ according to user historical reviews and item reviews from other users.
%

\subsection{GRPO}
\begin{figure}[t!]
\begin{center}
\includegraphics[width=8cm,height=2.5cm]{GRPO_0607_v2.png}
\caption{Overall framework of GRPO used in RecCoT.}
\label{grpo}
\end{center}
\vspace{-0.5cm}
\end{figure}
%
GRPO is a reinforcement learning method that directly uses the average reward of multiple sampling outputs of the same group as the baseline, which significantly reduces the use of training resources and simplifies the training process.
%
Specifically, for each problem $q$, GRPO samples a set of CoTs $\mathbf{\left\{o_{1}, o_{2}, \cdots, o_{G}\right\}}$ from the old policy $\mathbf{{\pi}_{{\theta}_{old}}}$, and then optimizes the policy model by maximizing the following objective function:
%
% 
%
\begin{equation} 
\footnotesize
\begin{aligned}
&\mathcal{J}_{G R P O}(\theta)=
\min \Bigl(\frac{\pi_{\theta}\left(o_{i} \mid q\right)}{\pi_{\theta_{o l d}}\left(o_{i} \mid q\right)} A_{i}, \operatorname{clip}\Bigl(\frac{\pi_{\theta}\left(o_{i} 
\mid q\right)}{\pi_{\theta_{o l d}}\left(o_{i} \mid q\right)}, 1-\varepsilon, 1+\varepsilon\Bigr) A_{i}\Bigr)\\
&\quad \quad \quad \quad \quad \quad- \beta \mathbb{D}_{K L}\left(\pi_{\theta} \| \pi_{r e f}\right),
\end{aligned}
\label{eqgrpo1}
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
\footnotesize
\mathbb{D}_{K L}\left(\pi_{\theta} \| \pi_{r e f}\right)=\frac{\pi_{r e f}\left(o_{i} \mid q\right)}{\pi_{\theta}\left(o_{i} \mid q\right)}-\log \frac{\pi_{r e f}\left(o_{i} \mid q\right)}{\pi_{\theta}\left(o_{i} \mid q\right)}-1,
\label{eqgrpo2}
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
\footnotesize
A_{i}=\frac{r_{i}-\operatorname{mean}\left(\left\{r_{1}, r_{2}, \cdots, r_{G}\right\}\right)}{\operatorname{std}\left(\left\{r_{1}, r_{2}, \cdots, r_{G}\right\}\right)}.
\label{eqgrpo3}
\end{equation}
% 
where $q$ is input tokens, $o_{i}$ is the CoT results, $A_{i}$ is the normalized rewards of CoT $o_{i}$ within each group $\{o_{1}\dots,o_{G}\}$.
%
In this way, the new policy model can increase the probability of higher reward CoT.
%
The figure~\ref{grpo} illustrates the framework of GRPO in this paper.
%

\section{Methodology}
\begin{figure*}[t]
\begin{center}
\includegraphics[width=16cm,height=4.8cm]{overall_v2.png}
\caption{Cascading workflow of the proposed RecCoT.}
\label{method}
\end{center}
\vspace{-0.5cm}
\end{figure*}
%
The architecture of our method RecCoT is illustrated in Figure~\ref{method}.
%
In part (a), we utilize LoRA to fine-tune a larger Qwen, adapting it to specific datasets and outputting it in a given format.
%
In part (b), we combine interpretable reasoning content from part(a) with original reviews as input to fine-tune a smaller BERT. 
%
The resulting CLS representations are then cached in a storage to support downstreaming RecSys. 
%
In part (c), these stacked CLS vectors are processed through attention layers and fed into a contrastive learning module to generate final recommendation scores.

\subsection{CoT Generation.}
%
To ensure that the high-quality CoTs generated by the Qwen model under the long-tail distirbution In RecSys scenario.
%
We first introduce a multi-reward system that uses three reward functions to constrain CoT following Deepseek R1~\cite{deepseekr1}.

\textbf{Format Reward.}
%
We implemented regular expression-based pattern matching to validate whether the output CoT adheres to predefined JSON format.
%
If compliance with the predefined JSON format, the $R_{\mathrm{format}} \,=\, 1$; otherwise, it is assigned 0.

\textbf{Frequency-aware Accuracy Reward.}
%
To alleviate the long-tail problem, we devise a novel reward strategy that: scores of high-frequency categories will decrease, and the scores of low-frequency categories will increase, and we further encourage the model to output float results for relative value comparison
%
Specifically, our piecewise reward function is defined as: 
%
%
\begin{equation}
\footnotesize
\begin{split}
R_{\mathrm{predict}} &= f \cdot \left[\texttt{Base}(e) + \texttt{Penalty}(e) \right],\quad e = \mathit{\hat{r} - r},\\
\quad \quad \quad & \texttt{Base}(e) = \exp(-e^2), \quad  \\
\texttt{Penalty}(e) &= 
\begin{cases} 
-\lambda \cdot \left( \exp(\mu \cdot |e|) - 1 \right) \quad  &  e < 0  \\
-\gamma \cdot \left( 1 - \frac{\exp(-\kappa \cdot e)}{1 + e} \right) \quad &  e \geq 0 
\end{cases} \\
\end{split}
\end{equation}
%
where $f$ is the occurrence frequency of rating category, $r$ is the ground-truth rating, $\mathit{\hat{r}}$ is the predicted rating, $\lambda$ and $\mu$ are underestimation penalty factor and $\gamma$ and $\kappa$ are the overestimation penalty factor.
%
Our reward curves are showing in Figure~\ref{grpo}.



\textbf{Analysis Quality Reward.}
%
Quantify the logical depth of CoT through semantic integrity indicators.
%
\begin{equation}
\footnotesize
R_{\text{quality}} = \frac{\ell_{\text{CoT}} - \ell_{\min}}{\ell_{\max} - \ell_{\min}}
\end{equation}
%
where $\mathbf{\ell_{\text{CoT}}}$ is the length of the current CoT text, $\ell_{\text{min}}=100$
and $\ell_{\text{max}}=200$ are the preset minimum and maximum effective length thresholds, respectively. 
%
This function ensures that CoT avoids information redundancy and has sufficient analytical depth.



Finally, we could utilize them to serve as reward system to conduct the GRPO (in Eq.(\ref{eqgrpo1}-\ref{eqgrpo3})) to finetune LLMs (e.g., Qwen) to generate CoT for each review with 2-fold setting.



\subsection{Semantic Embedding Alignment}
\textbf{BERT SFT}. After obtaining the CoT of each review, we next fuse CoT and review into a semantic embedding and cache them to a storage, to release the computation pressure from RecSys model.
% 
%
Specifically, we first fine-tune the BERT as follows:
%
%
\begin{equation}
\footnotesize
\mathbf{CLS}_{u,i} = \texttt{BERT}(o_{u,i} \oplus t_{u,i})
\label{eq:concat}
\end{equation}
%
where $o_{u,i}$ and $t_{u,i}$ are CoT and original review texts, $\mathbf{CLS}_{u,i}$ is the compressed semantic embedding.
%
Afterward, we use the MSE loss to fine-tune the BERT:
 \begin{equation}
 \footnotesize
\begin{split}
\mathcal{L}_\mathrm{BERT}=(\hat{r}_\mathrm{u,i} - r_\mathrm{u,i})^2,\quad \hat{r}_\mathrm{u,i}=\texttt{MLP}(\mathbf{CLS}_{u,i})
\label{L_pre}
\end{split}
\end{equation}
%
where \texttt{MLP} is a projector, $\hat{r}_\mathrm{u,i}$ denotes predicted rating.


\textbf{Embedding Cache}. 
After the BERT convergenced, we next cache the semantic embedding for each review as follows:
% 
\begin{equation}
\footnotesize
\begin{split}
\texttt{Cache\_Store}_u \xleftarrow{}(\mathbf{CLS}_{u}^1, \mathbf{CLS}_{u}^2, \dots, \mathbf{CLS}_{u}^m) \\
\texttt{Cache\_Store}_i \xleftarrow{}(\mathbf{CLS}_{i}^1, \mathbf{CLS}_{i}^2, \dots, \mathbf{CLS}_{i}^n)
\end{split}
\label{cls}
\end{equation}
where the \texttt{Cache\_Store} saves all reviews embedding, the $\mathbf{CLS}_{u}^*$/$\mathbf{CLS}_{i}^*$ are the semantic embeddings of corresponding user/item, and $m$, $n$ represent the number of reviews from user $u$ and item $i$.




\subsection{Down-streaming RecSys}
On top of the \texttt{Cache\_Store}, in RecSys model, we could directly select the semantic embedding without re-computation as follows:
\begin{equation}
\footnotesize
\begin{split}
\mathbf{H}_u &= (\mathbf{CACHE}_u^1, \ldots, \mathbf{CACHE}_u^m) \xleftarrow{} \texttt{Cache\_Store}_u \\
\mathbf{H}_i &= (\mathbf{CACHE}_i^1, \ldots, \mathbf{CACHE}_i^n)  \xleftarrow{} \texttt{Cache\_Store}_i
\end{split}
\label{item_stack}
\end{equation}
%
%
where the $\mathbf{H}_u\in \mathbb{R}^{m\times d}$ and $\mathbf{H}_i \in \mathbb{R}^{n\times d}$ are the Semantic embedding results of user/item, $d = 768$. 
%
According to the historical semantic sequence, we next conduct cross-attention layers to integrate the semantic information $\mathbf{H}_u$ into the item ID representation $\mathbf{p}_{i}$, versus versa for user ID representation $\mathbf{p}_{u}$. 
\begin{equation}
\small
\begin{split}
\mathbf{v}_i = \text{Attention}(\mathbf{p}_i, \mathbf{H}_u, \mathbf{H}_u), \ \ \mathbf{v}_u = \text{Attention}(\mathbf{p}_u, \mathbf{H}_i, \mathbf{H}_i)
\end{split}
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{v}_i$ and $\mathbf{v}_u$ are the updated item and user representations.
To model user-item relationships ignored by attention, we apply contrastive learning. 
%
For each updated representation $\mathbf{v}_i$/$\mathbf{v}_u$ (anchor), its interacted user/item representation $\mathbf{p}^+$ serves as positive, while a random in-batch representation $\mathbf{p}^-$ as negative. 
%
We minimize:
\begin{equation}
\footnotesize
\mathcal{L}\mathrm{cl} = \max\left(0, \Delta + \mathrm{D}(\mathbf{v}, \mathbf{p}^+) - \mathrm{D}(\mathbf{v}, \mathbf{p}^-)\right)
\end{equation}
where $\mathrm{D}=||\cdot||^2_2$, and margin $\Delta$ enforces separation robustness.
%
Finally, we condouct the MSE loss to optimize the down-streaming RecSys model as follows:
\begin{equation}
\footnotesize
\begin{split}
\hat{r}_\mathrm{u,i} = \texttt{MLP}(\mathbf{v}_u \oplus \mathbf{v}_i), \ \ 
\mathcal{L}_\mathrm{rec} = (\hat{r}_\mathrm{u,i} - r_\mathrm{u,i})^2,
\end{split}
\label{L_rec}
\end{equation}
where the $\mathcal{L}_\mathrm{rec} + \mathcal{L}\mathrm{cl}$ is used to train the RecSys model.

