\subsection{Discussion of User Review Length}
The length of reviews exhibits a significantly nonlinear impact on model performance. 
%
We selected the domain-representative Musical Instruments dataset to investigate this phenomenon.
%
Within the shortest review range (0-127 characters), the model exhibited its MSE of 0.2825. 
%
As review length increased to the 128-255 character range, the MSE decreased to 0.2464, reaching its lowest point of 0.2297 in the 256-511 character range. 
%
Notably, when reviews extended into the long-text range of 512-1024 characters, the MSE showed a significant rebound, surging to a peak of 0.3501.
%
This fluctuating trend indicates that medium-length reviews (256-511 characters) are most conducive to optimizing model performance, while overly long reviews may introduce redundant information or increased noise, paradoxically reducing modeling efficiency. 

\section{Conclusions}
%
This paper introduces a simple yet effective approach named RecCoT, which includes two major components.
%
1)Slow Interpretable CoT Generation via Larger LLM, we proposed a reward-constrained reasoning paradigm.
2)Fast Semantic Embedding Alignment via Smaller LLM, we use BERT to obtain semantic embeddings and use contrastive learning for alignment.
%
Extensive experiments reveal that RecCoT surpasses existing SOTA methods, and in-depth analyses underscore the effectiveness of our model components.
%
In the future, we will find more policies to prevent reward hacking.

