	\begin{abstract}
		The class $\totp$ consists of functions that count the number of all paths of a nondeterministic polynomial-time Turing machine. In this paper, we give a second definition of the class $\totp$ in terms of certificates and verifiers, and present a few natural closure properties of this class. From the second definition of $\totp$ it follows that $\totp = \sp$ if and only if $\p = \np$. 
		We also prove that the closure of $\totp$ under left composition with the class $\fp_{+}$ is equivalent to $\totp = \fp_{+}$ and $\p = \pp$, and give examples of $\fp_{+}$-functions such that if $\totp$ is closed under composition with them, then it is closed under composition with $\fp_{+}$.							


		\keywords{computational complexity \and TotP \and counting problems  \and closure properties \and \#P.}	

	\end{abstract}
	%
	%
	%


	\section{Introduction}


	The class $\sp$, which was introduced in \cite{val79}, consists of functions that count the number of accepting paths of a nondeterministic polynomial-time Turing machine (NPTM). Also, the class $\sp$ can be characterized as a class of functions that are equal to the number of solutions for problems from the class $\np$. The class $\totp$, introduced in \cite{kia01}, is a subclass of $\sp$ containing functions that count the number of all paths of an NPTM. In this paper, we give a second definition of $\totp$, in which $\totp$, as well as the class $\sp$, is described as a class of functions equal to the number of solutions for $\np$-problems of a certain type.
	Also, $\totp$ coincides with the closure under Karp reductions of the class of self-reducible functions from the class $\spe$ \cite{pag06}, and in \cite{bam17} $\totp$ is characterized using interval size functions. Examples of problems from the class $\totp$ are $\textsc{\#Perfect Matchings}$, $\textsc{\#DNF-Sat}$, and $\textsc{NonNegative Permanent}$, which are $\sp$-complete with respect to polynomial-time 1-Turing reductions (for other examples, see \cite{pag06,ant22}). $\totp$-complete problems with respect to Karp reductions are given in \cite{ant22}. In \cite{bak20}, the relationship of $\totp$ with classes of approximable functions is studied. Logical characterizations of $\totp$ and some of its subclasses are given in \cite{ach23,are20,bak20}. \\

	In this paper, we study the closure properties of $\totp$. $\totp$ has natural closure properties, for example, it is closed under addition, multiplication, and subtraction by one (see Definition 3.1) \cite{bak23}. In Section 3, we give a few more closure properties of $\totp$ that $\sp$ and $\gapp$ have \cite{bei92,fen94}, and also properties that are unknown to hold for $\sp$ and $\gapp$. 

	In Section 5, we study the closure of $\totp$ under composition with $FP_{+}$ (i.e., whether $\fp_{+} \circ \totp \subseteq \totp$), and prove that this statement is equivalent to $\totp = \fp_{+}$ and $\p = \pp$. For the class $\sp$, being closed under left composition with $\fp_{+}$ is equivalent to $\pp = \up$ and $\mathsf{CH} = \up$ \cite{ogi93}; for $\gapp$ is equivalent\footnote{For $\gapp$, the closure under operation $\ominus 1$ is also equivalent to $\pp = \spp$ \cite{thi94}. For the class $\sp$, it is only known that the closure under operation $\ominus 1$ implies $\np \subseteq \spp$ \cite{ogi93}.} to $\pp = \spp$ and $\mathsf{CH} = \spp$ \cite{ogi93,gup91}.

	We also give examples of $\fp_{+}$-functions of several arguments such that if $\totp$ is closed under composition with them, then $\totp$ is closed under composition with $\fp_{+}$. Similar results for the class $\sp$ were presented in \cite{ogi93}. 

	\section{Preliminaries}

	In this paper, we use standard definitions of a deterministic and a nondeterministic Turing machine \cite{sip12}. We use a finite alphabet $\Sigma = \{0, 1\}$. All languages are subsets of $\Sigma^{*}$. $|x|$ denotes the length of the string $x \in \Sigma^{*}$. 
	%All functions (that are computed by Turing machines) have the form $\Sigma^{*} \rightarrow \Sigma^{*}$ by default. Sometimes the arguments of functions can be natural numbers or integers, and also pairs of words from $\Sigma^{*}$ (in these cases, it is assumed that the algorithm takes a string from $\Sigma^{*}$ as an input and translates it into the desired format using a polynomially computable bijective function). 


	\begin{Definition}
		Let $M$ be a nondeterministic polynomial-time Turing machine (NPTM).

		1. $acc_{M}(x)$ is the number of accepting paths of $M$ on input $x \in \Sigma^{*}$.

		2. $rej_{M}(x)$ is the number of rejecting paths of $M$ on input $x \in \Sigma^{*}$.

		3. $gap_{M}(x) = acc_{M}(x) - rej_{M}(x)$.

		4. $Total_{M}(x) = acc_{M}(x) + rej_{M}(x)$.

		5. $tot_{M}(x) = Total_{M}(x) - 1$. 
	\end{Definition}

	\begin{Definition}
		1. \textnormal{\cite{val79}} $\sp = \{acc_{M} \ | \ M$ is a NPTM$\}$.

		2. \textnormal{\cite{fen94}} $\gapp = \{gap_{M} \ | \ M$ is a NPTM$\}$.

		3. \textnormal{\cite{kia01}} $\totp = \{tot_{M} \ | \ M$ is a NPTM$\}$.\footnote{The "-1" in the definition of $\totp$ allows these functions to take a zero value.}

		4. \textnormal{\cite{pag06}} $\spe = \{f \ | \ f \in \sp \wedge \{x \ | \ f(x) > 0\} \in \p\}$.

		5. $\fp = \{f: \Sigma^{*} \rightarrow \mathbb{Z} \ | \ f$ is computable in polynomial time$\}$.

		6. $\fp_{+} = \{f \ | \ f \in \fp$ and $f \geqslant 0\}$.
	\end{Definition}

	An NPTM $M$ is in \textit{normal form} if at each nondeterministic step $M$ chooses between two possible paths and for any $x \in \Sigma^{*}$ on each path $M(x)$ takes a number of nondeterministic steps equal to some fixed polynomial $p(|x|)$. If an NPTM $M$ is in normal form, then for any $x \in \Sigma^{*}$: $Total_{M}(x) = 2^{p(|x|)}$. 
	For any NPTM $M$ there exists an NPTM $M'$ in normal form such that for any $x \in \Sigma^{*}$: $acc_{M}(x) = acc_{M'}(x)$. %: for this $M'$ repeats the action of $M$, and then generates the missing number of rejecting paths. 
	Thus, the class $\sp$ does not change if we replace an arbitrary NPTM in the definition with an NPTM in normal form. \\

	%Thus, in the definition of $\sp$ an arbitrary NPTM can replaced with an NPTM in normal form. \\
	%In the definition of $\totp$ an arbitrary NPTM should be used. \\ %To define the class $\gapp$, we also cannot use an NPTM in normal form (since $gap_{M}(x)$ of such a machine is always even), but for any function $f$ such that $f(x) = 2 \cdot gap_{M}(x)$ for some NPTM $M$, there exists an NPTM $N$ in normal form such that $f(x) = gap_{N}(x)$ \cite{fen94}. \\


	The class $\sp$ has a second definition using polynomial verifiers: 

	\begin{Proposition}
		The class $\sp$ consists of all functions $f$ for which there exist a polynomial $p$ and a polynomial-time algorithm $V$ such that for any $x \in \Sigma^{*}$: $f(x) = |\{y \ | \ |y| = p(|x|) \wedge V(x, y) = 1\}|$.
	\end{Proposition} 

	In Proposition 2.4, we give a second definition of the class $\totp$ in terms of certificates. For this, an additional condition is added to the definition of the class $\sp$: there exists a polynomial-time algorithm that determines whether it is possible to extend an arbitrary string to a certificate that the verifier accepts. 

	\begin{Proposition} 
		The class $\totp$ consists of all functions $f$ for which there exist a polynomial $p$ and a polynomial-time algorithm $V$ such that:

		1. For any $x \in \Sigma^{*}$: $f(x) = |\{y \ | \ |y| = p(|x|) \wedge V(x, y) = 1\}|$.

		2. The language %\footnote{Since this language is in $\np$ it follows that $\totp = \sp$ if and only if $\p = \np$.} 
		$\{(x, z) \in \Sigma^{*} \times \Sigma^{*}\ | \ \exists y (|zy| = p(|x|) \wedge V(x, zy) = 1)\}$ is in the class $\p$, where $zy$ denotes the concatenation of strings $z$ and $y$. 
	\end{Proposition}

	\begin{proof}
		First suppose that $f$ is in $\totp$, thus $f(x) = tot_{M}$ for some NPTM $M$. Consider a polynomial-time algorithm $V$ that accepts $(x, y)$ if $y$ is a path of $M(x)$ that is not the lexicographically largest path in $M(x)$, where paths of $M(x)$ are encoded so that the length of  the certificate $y$ equals some fixed polynomial $p(|x|)$. Then there exists a polynomial-time algorithm that determines whether a given string is the beginning of some path in $M(x)$ that is not the lexicographically largest path in $M(x)$. \\

		Now suppose that for a function $f$ there exist a polynomial $p$ and a polynomial-time algorithm $V$ such that the above conditions hold. 

		\noindent Let $M$ be the NPTM that on input $x \in \Sigma^{*}$:
		\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.5cm]
			\item[(a)] If the empty string cannot be extended to a certificate that $V$ accepts, $M$ halts.
			\item[(b)] $M$ takes one nondeterministic step and halts on the first path.
			\item[(c)] On the second path, $M$ nondeterministically guesses the certificate $y$. Initially, $y$ is the empty string. Then, if it can be extented with both 0 and 1, $M$ makes one nondeterministic step and on one path replaces $y$ with $y1$, and on the other path replaces $y$ with $y0$. If $y$ can only be extended in 1 way, then $M$ does not take a nondeterministic step.
			\item[(d)] If the length of $y$ becomes equal to $p(|x|)$ (in this case $y$ becomes a certificate that $V$ accepts), $M$ halts.
		\end{enumerate}
		We have $f(x) = tot_{M}(x)$. \qed 
	\end{proof}

	The second definition of $\totp$ given in Proposition 2.4 can be used to determine that a function belongs to this class. For example, for the problems $\textsc{\#Perfect Matchings}$, $\textsc{\#DNF-Sat}$, $\textsc{\#MonotoneSat}$, $\textsc{\#NonCliques}$, \\ $\textsc{\#NonIndependent Sets}$, $\textsc{\#NFA}$ \cite{pag06,alv93} it is easy to determine whether an arbitrary string can be extended to a certificate\footnote{In \cite[p.413]{val79b}, Valiant observes that many $\sp$-complete problems have the second property from Proposition 2.4. It follows from Fact 7 on p.413 that for all $\totp$-problems the corresponding relation is P-enumerable, which means that there exists an algorithm that for any $x \in \Sigma^{*}$ prints all certificates in time $p(|x|)\cdot N$, where $N$ is the number of certificates and $p$ is some polynomial. For all $\sp$-problems the same is not true unless $\p = \up$ \cite{val79}.}. Another way to understand that a function belongs to $\totp$ is to ensure that it belongs to $\spe$ and it is self-reducible. In \cite{pag06}, it is shown that $\totp$ contains all self-reducible $\spe$-problems. 

	\begin{Proposition} [\cite{pag06}]
		$\totp$ is the closure under Karp reductions of the class of self-reducible $\spe$ functions. 
	\end{Proposition}

	Below are the known relations between classes of functions: 

	\begin{Proposition} [\cite{fen94}]
		1. $\fp_{+} \subseteq \sp \subseteq \gapp$.

		2. $\gapp = \sp - \sp = \sp - \fp = \fp - \sp$. 
	\end{Proposition}

	\begin{Proposition} 
		1. \textnormal{\cite{pag06}} $\fp_{+} \subseteq \totp \subseteq \spe \subseteq \sp$. All inclusions are proper %\footnote{It follows from Proposition 2.4 that $\totp = \sp$ if and only if $\p = \np$.}
		unless $\p = \np$.

		2. \textnormal{\cite{bak23}} $\gapp = \totp - \fp$. 
	\end{Proposition}

	If $\p = \np$, then the second condition in Proposition 2.4 holds for all $\sp$-functions. This gives the following corollary: 

	\begin{Corollary} 
		$\totp = \sp$ if and only if $\p = \np$. 
	\end{Corollary} 

	Next we give definitions of classes of languages that are defined using counting functions: 

	\begin{Definition}
		1. \textnormal{\cite{coo71,lev73}} The class $\np$ consists of all languages $L$ for which there exists a function $f \in \sp$ such that for any $x \in \Sigma^{*}$: 
		\begin{align*}
			x \in L \Leftrightarrow f(x) > 0.
		\end{align*}
		2. \textnormal{\cite{val76}} The class $\up$ consists of all languages $L$ for which there exists a function $f \in \sp$ such that for any $x \in \Sigma^{*}$:
		\begin{align*}
			x \in L \Leftrightarrow f(x) = 1, \\
			x \notin L \Leftrightarrow f(x) = 0.
		\end{align*}
		3. \textnormal{\cite{sim75,wag86}} The class $\cp$ consists of all languages $L$ for which there exist functions $f \in \sp$ and $g \in \fp$ such that for any $x \in \Sigma^{*}$:
		\begin{align*}
			x \in L \Leftrightarrow f(x) = g(x).
		\end{align*}
		4. \textnormal{\cite{sim75,gil77}} The class $\pp$ consists of all languages $L$ for which there exist functions $f \in \sp$ and $g \in \fp$ such that for any $x \in \Sigma^{*}$:
		\begin{align*}
			x \in L \Leftrightarrow f(x) > g(x).
		\end{align*}
	\end{Definition}
	%In \cite{fen94}, the equivalent definitions of the classes $\cp$ and $\pp$ that use $\gapp$-functions are given: \\

	%\noindent \textbf{Proposition 2.9. \cite{fen94}} 1. A language $L$ is in the class $\cp$ if and only if there exists a function $f \in \gapp$ such that for any $x \in \Sigma^{*}$:
	%\[x \in L \Leftrightarrow f(x) = 0.\]

	%2. A language $L$ is in the class $\pp$ if and only if there exists a function $f \in \gapp$ such that for any $x \in \Sigma^{*}$:
	%\[x \in L \Leftrightarrow f(x) > 0.\] 

	\section{Closure properties of TotP}

	In this section, we present closure properties of $\totp$ that complement the results from \cite{bak23} (Proposition 3.2). 

	\begin{Definition}
		For integers $a$ and $b$, the \textit{decrement} operation is defined as follows: \\
		\[a \ominus b =
		\begin{cases}
			a - b, &\text{if $a \geqslant b$;}\\
			0, &\text{otherwise.}
		\end{cases}\] 
	\end{Definition}

	\begin{Proposition} [\cite{bak23}]
		1. For any functions $f, g \in \totp$, the functions $f + g$, $f \cdot g$, and $f \ominus 1$ are in $\totp$.

		2. Let $f \in \totp$. If there exists a polynomial $p$ such that for any $x \in \Sigma^{*}$: $f(x) \leqslant p(|x|)$, then $f \in \fp$. 
	\end{Proposition}

	Proposition 3.3 lists closure properties of $\totp$ that are not known to hold for $\sp$ and $\gapp$. 

	\begin{Proposition}
		For any functions $f \in \totp$ and $g \in \fp_{+}$, such that $g(x) \leqslant poly(|x|)$:
		\begin{enumerate}
			\item The languages $\{x \ | \ f(x) = g(x)\}$ and $\{x \ | \ f(x) > g(x)\}$ are in $\p$.
			\item The function $f(x) \ominus g(x)$ is in $\totp$. 
		\end{enumerate}
	\end{Proposition}

	\begin{proof}
		Let $f(x) = tot_{M}(x)$ for an NPTM $M$. To decide the languages $\{x \ | \ f(x) = g(x)\}$ and $\{x \ | \ f(x) > g(x)\}$, the algorithm simulates the first $g(x) + 2$ paths of $M$ on input $x$. Let $N$ be the NPTM that on input $x \in \Sigma^{*}$: if $M(x)$ has at least $g(x) + 1$ paths, guesses 1 path of $M(x)$ that is lexicographically larger than the first $g(x)$ paths. Then $tot_{N}(x) = f(x) \ominus g(x)$. \qed
	\end{proof}

	The properties from Proposition 3.4 also hold for the classes $\sp$ \cite{bei92} and $\gapp$ \cite{fen94}. 

	\begin{Proposition}
		For any functions $f \in \totp$ and $g \in \fp_{+}$, such that $g(x) \leqslant poly(|x|)$:
		\begin{enumerate}
			\item The function $f(x)^{g(x)}$ is in $\totp$.
			\item The function $\binom{f(x)}{g(x)}$ is in $\totp$. 
		\end{enumerate}
	\end{Proposition}

	\begin{proof}
		Let $f(x) = tot_{M}(x)$ for an NPTM $M$. \\

		\noindent 1. Let $N$ be the NPTM that on input $x \in \Sigma^{*}$:
		\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.5cm]
			\item[(a)] If $f(x) = 0$, $N$ halts.
			\item[(b)] If $g(x) = 0$, $N$ takes one nondeterministic step and halts on both paths.
			\item[(c)] N takes one nondeterministic step and halts on the first path. On the second path, $N$ guesses $g(x)$ paths of $M(x)$ (with duplicates) that are lexicographically larger than the first path of $M(x)$.
		\end{enumerate}
		$tot_{N}(x) = f(x)^{g(x)}$. \\

		\noindent 2. Let $N$ be the NPTM that on input $x \in \Sigma^{*}$:
		\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.5cm]
			\item[(a)] If $f(x) = 0$, $N$ halts.
			\item[(b)] $N$ simulates the first $g(x) + 1$ paths of $M(x)$.
			\item[(c)] If $M(x)$ has at least $g(x) + 1$ paths, $N$ takes one nondeterministic step and halts on the first path. On the second path, $N$ guesses a set of $g(x)$ distinct paths of $M(x)$ that are lexicographically larger than the first path of $M(x)$.
		\end{enumerate}
		$tot_{N}(x) = \binom{f(x)}{g(x)}$. \qed
	\end{proof}

	The classes $\sp$ and $\gapp$ are closed under polynomial summation and polynomial product \cite{bei92,fen94}. The same is true for $\totp$ (Proposition 3.5). 

	\begin{Proposition}
		For any functions $f \in \totp$ and $g \in \fp_{+}$, such that $g(x) \leqslant poly(|x|)$:
		\begin{enumerate}
			\item The function $s(x) = \sum_{0 \leqslant y \leqslant g(x)} f(x, y)$ is in $\totp$.
			\item The function $m(x) = \prod_{0 \leqslant y \leqslant g(x)} f(x, y)$ is in $\totp$. 
		\end{enumerate}
	\end{Proposition}

	\begin{proof}
		Let $f(x, y) = tot_{M}(x, y)$ for an NPTM $M$. \\

		\noindent 1. Let $N$ be the NPTM that on input $x \in \Sigma^{*}$:
		\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.5cm]
			\item[] For $y$ in range $\{0, ..., g(x)\}$:
			\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.5cm]
				\item[(1)] If $f(x, y) > 0$, $N$ takes one nondeterministic step. On the first path, $N$ guesses 1 path of $M(x, y)$ that is lexicographically larger than the first path of $M(x, y)$.
				\item[(2)] If $y = g(x)$, $N$ halts on the second path. Otherwise, $N$ executes the next iteration of the loop on the second path.
			\end{enumerate}
		\end{enumerate}
		$tot_{N}(x) = \sum_{0 \leqslant y \leqslant g(x)} f(x, y)$. \\

		\noindent 2. Let $N$ be the NPTM that on input $x \in \Sigma^{*}$:
		\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.5cm]
			\item[(a)] For $y$ in range $\{0, ..., g(x)\}$:
			\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1cm]
				\item[] If $f(x, y) = 0$, $N$ halts.
			\end{enumerate}
			\item[(b)] $N$ takes one nondeterministic step, halts on the first path, and continues on the second path.
			\item[(c)] For $y$ in range $\{0, ..., g(x)\}$:
			\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1cm]
				\item[] $N$ guesses 1 path of $M(x, y)$ that is lexicographically larger than the first path of $M(x, y)$.
			\end{enumerate}
		\end{enumerate}
		$tot_{N}(x) = \prod_{0 \leqslant y \leqslant g(x)} f(x, y)$. \qed
	\end{proof} 

	The classes $\sp$ and $\gapp$ are also closed under exponential summation \cite{bei92,fen94}. In Proposition 3.6 we prove that $\totp$ doesn't have this property unless $\p = \np$. 

	\begin{Proposition} 
		The following statements are equivalent:
		\begin{enumerate}
			\item For any functions $f \in \totp$ and $g \in \fp_{+}$, such that $g(x) \leqslant poly(|x|)$, the function $s(x) = \sum_{|y| \leqslant g(x)} f(x, y)$ is in $\totp$.
			\item $\p = \np$. 
		\end{enumerate}
	\end{Proposition}

	\begin{proof} 
		We will use the equivalent $\totp = \sp$ instead of $\p = \np$. If $\totp = \sp$, then the first statement holds since $\sp$ has this property. Let $h$ be a function in $\sp$, thus there exist an $\fp$-function $V$$:\Sigma^{*} \times \Sigma^{*} \rightarrow \{0, 1\}$ and a polynomial $p$ such that for any $x \in \Sigma^{*}$: $h(x) = |\{y \ | \ |y| \leqslant p(|x|) \wedge V(x, y) = 1\}|$. By assumption, the function $h(x) = \sum_{|y| \leqslant p(|x|)} V(x, y)$ is in $\totp$. \qed
	\end{proof}

	\section{Characterization of GapP with TotP functions}

	Recall that $\gapp = \totp - \fp$ (Proposition 2.7). In Proposition 4.2 we give a more precise characterization of the class $\gapp$ with use of $\totp$-functions. In the statement of the second part of Proposition 4.1 the class $\totp$ can be replaced by the class $\mathsf{SpanL}$ \cite{alv93} (the proof is given in \cite{hem95}). $\mathsf{SpanL}$ is a subclass %\footnote{It follows from Proposition 2.4, because an $\mathsf{NL}$-algorithm can determine whether an arbitrary string can be extended to a valid output of an $\mathsf{NL}$-transducer.}
	of $\totp$, so the proof of the second part of Proposition 4.1 also follows from \cite{hem95}.  In this section, we give a simpler proof of this statement. 

	\begin{Lemma}
		For any NPTM $M$ and any polynomial $p$ such that for any $x \in \Sigma^{*}$ $p(|x|)$ is greater than the running time of $M(x)$:

		1. \textnormal{\cite{kia01}} There exists a function $g_{1} \in \totp$ such that for any $x \in \Sigma^{*}$: $acc_{M}(x) = g_{1}(x) - 2^{p(|x|)}$.

		2. There exists a function $g_{2} \in \totp$ such that for any $x \in \Sigma^{*}$: $acc_{M}(x) = 2^{p(|x|)} - g_{2}(x)$. 
	\end{Lemma}

	\begin{proof}
		1. We repeat the proof from \cite{kia01}. Let $M'$ be a normal form NPTM such that $acc_{M}(x) = acc_{M'}(x)$ and $Total_{M'}(x) = 2^{p(|x|)}$. Consider the NPTM $N$ that repeats the action of $M'$, but instead of going to an accepting state, it generates 2 accepting paths.

		\noindent $acc_{M}(x) = Total_{N}(x) - Total_{M'}(x) = (tot_{N}(x) + 1) - 2^{p(|x|)}$.

		2. Let $M'$ be a normal form NPTM such that $acc_{M}(x) = acc_{M'}(x)$ and $Total_{M'}(x) = 2^{p(|x|) - 1}$. Consider the NPTM $N$ that repeats the action of $M'$ but instead of going to a rejecting state, it generates 2 rejecting paths.

		\noindent $acc_{M}(x) = 2 \cdot Total_{M'}(x) - Total_{N}(x) = 2^{p(|x|)} - (tot_{N}(x) + 1)$. \qed
	\end{proof}

	\begin{Proposition}
		For any function $f \in \gapp$ there exists a function $g \in \totp$ and a polynomial $p$ such that for any $x \in \Sigma^{*}$: $f(x) = g(x) - 2^{p(|x|)}$. 
	\end{Proposition}

	\begin{proof}
		Let $f(x) = acc_{M}(x) - acc_{N}(x)$ for some NPTMs $M$ and $N$. By Lemma 4.1, there exist functions $g_{1}, g_{2} \in \totp$ such that $acc_{M}(x) = g_{1}(x) - 2^{p(|x|)}$ and $acc_{N}(x) = 2^{p(|x|)} - g_{2}(x)$, where $p$ is any polynomial that is greater than the running time of $M$ and $N$.

		\noindent $f(x) = (g_{1}(x) - 2^{p(|x|)}) - (2^{p(|x|)} - g_{2}(x)) = (g_{1}(x) + g_{2}(x)) - 2^{p(|x|) + 1}$. \qed
	\end{proof}

	In Proposition 4.4, we give a characterization of the class $\cp$ that is used in the next section. The proof of Lemma 4.3 is given in \cite{for97}. 

	\begin{Lemma} [\cite{for97}]
		For any language $L \in \cp$, there exists a function $f \in \gapp$ such that for any $x \in \Sigma^{*}$:
		\begin{align*}
			x \in L \Leftrightarrow f(x) = 0, \\
			x \notin L \Leftrightarrow f(x) < 0.
		\end{align*}
	\end{Lemma}

	\begin{Proposition}
		For any language $L \in \cp$, there exist a function $g \in \totp$ and a polynomial $p$ such that for any $x \in \Sigma^{*}$:
		\begin{align*}
			x \in L \Leftrightarrow g(x) = 2^{p(|x|)}, \\
			x \notin L \Leftrightarrow g(x) < 2^{p(|x|)}. 
		\end{align*}
	\end{Proposition}

	The proof of Proposition 4.4 follows from Proposition 4.2 and Lemma 4.3. 

	\section{Closure under composition with FP$_{+}$}

	%The class $\totp$ is closed under parsimonious reductions

	Similar to the class $\sp$, the class $\totp$ is closed under right composition with $\fp_{+}$, thus $\totp \circ \fp_{+} \subseteq \totp$. In this section, we prove that the closure of $\totp$ under left composition with $\fp_{+}$ is equivalent to $\totp = \fp_{+}$ and $\pp = \p$. In the next theorem, we will also consider $\fp_{+}$-functions of several arguments. A function $h$ is in the class $\fp_{+} \circ (\totp \times ... \times \totp)$ if there exist functions $f \in \fp_{+}$ and $g_{1}, ..., g_{k} \in \totp$ such that for any $x \in \Sigma^{*}$: $h(x) = f(g_{1}(x), ..., g_{k}(x))$. 

	%\noindent \textbf{Lemma 5.1.} $\fp \circ \totp = \fp^{\totp[1]}$. \\

	%\noindent \textit{Proof.} Clearly, $\fp \circ \totp$ is a subset of $\fp^{\totp[1]}$. Let $f(x) \in \fp^{\totp[1]}$, thus there exist functions $h, s \in \fp$, $g \in \totp$ such that $f(x) = h(x, g(s(x)))$. Consider the polynomial time computable functions $d(``x_{1}...x_{n}") = ``10x_{1}x_{1}...x_{n}x_{n}"$ and $l(``x_{1}...x_{n}") = 2^{2|x_{1}...x_{n}| + 2}$. Let $t(x) = g(s(x)) \cdot l(x) + d(x)$. The function $t \in \totp$ on input $``x_{1}...x_{n}"$ returns $``g(s(x_{1}...x_{n}))10x_{1}x_{1}...x_{n}x_{n}"$. Therefore, $f(x) = r(t(x))$, where $r$ is a polynomial time computable function such that $r(``y10x_{1}x_{1}...x_{n}x_{n}") = h(``x_{1}...x_{n}"$$, y)$.
	%\vspace{-10pt}
	%\begin{flushright}
	%$\square$
	%\end{flushright}

	\begin{Theorem}
		The following statements are equivalent:
		\begin{enumerate}
			\item $\fp_{+} \circ \totp \subseteq \totp$.
			\item $\fp_{+} \circ (\totp \times ... \times \totp) \subseteq \totp$.
			\item $\totp = \fp_{+}$.
			\item $\pp = \p$. 
		\end{enumerate}
	\end{Theorem}

	\begin{proof}
		$(2 \Rightarrow 1)$: (1) is a special case of (2). \\

		\noindent $(1 \Rightarrow 4)$: Let $L = \{x \ | \ d(x) > 0\}$ and $d(x) = f(x) - g(x)$ for some functions $d \in \gapp$, $f \in \totp$, and $g \in \fp$.
		The function $h(x) =
		\begin{cases}
			1, &\text{$f(x) > g(x)$;}\\
			0, &\text{$\textnormal{otherwise}$.}
		\end{cases}$ is in $\fp_{+}^{\totp[1]}$, which coincides with the class $\fp_{+} \circ \totp$. By assumption, $h \in \totp$. Hence, by Proposition 3.2, $h \in \fp_{+}$ and $L \in \p$. \\

		\noindent $(4 \Rightarrow 3)$: $\sp = \fp_{+}$ is equivalent to $\pp = \p$ \cite{bal97}, and is also equivalent to $\totp = \fp_{+}$. \\

		\noindent $(3 \Rightarrow 2)$: If $\totp = \fp_{+}$, then $\fp_{+} \circ (\totp \times ... \times \totp) = \fp_{+} \circ (\fp_{+} \times ... \times \fp_{+}) \subseteq \fp_{+}$. \qed
	\end{proof}

	In the next theorem, we give examples of $\fp_{+}$-functions of several agruments such that if $\totp$ is closed under composition with them, then it is closed under composition with all functions in $\fp_{+}$. Similar results for the class $\sp$ were obtained in \cite{ogi93}. 

	\begin{Definition}
		1. $span(a_{1}, ..., a_{k})$ is the number of distinct elements in a set $\{a_{1}, ..., a_{k}\}$.

		2. $plu(a_{1}, ..., a_{k})$ is the set of most frequently occurring elements in a set $\{a_{1}, ..., a_{k}\}$. 
	\end{Definition}

	\begin{Theorem}
		The following statements are equivalent:
		\begin{enumerate}
			\item $\fp_{+} \circ (\totp \times ... \times \totp) \subseteq \totp$.
			\item $\cp = \p$.
			\item For any functions $f\in \totp$, $g \in \fp_{+ }$, the function $f(x) \ominus g(x)$ is in $\totp$.
			\item For any functions $f\in \totp$, $g \in \fp_{+ }$, such that $g(x) \neq 0$, the function %\footnote{In \cite{ogi93}, it is proved that if $\sp$ is closed under division by 2, then $\mathsf{PH} \subseteq \pp$. The same proof can be repeated for $\totp$ \cite{ogi93}, because the class $\oplus\p$ can be defined with $\totp$-functions instead of $\sp$-functions \cite{bak23}. In \cite{bak23}, it is proved that for every $k$ the class $\mathsf{Mod}_{\mathsf{k}}\p$ can be defined with use of $\totp$-functions.}
			$\lfloor f(x) / g(x) \rfloor$ is in $\totp$.
			\item For any functions $f_{1}, ..., f_{k} \in \totp$ the function $span(f_{1}, ..., f_{k})$ is in $\totp$.
			\item For any functions $f_{1}, ..., f_{k} \in \totp$ there exists a function $g \in \totp$ such that for any $x$: $g(x) \in plu(f_{1}(x), ..., f_{k}(x))$. 
		\end{enumerate}
	\end{Theorem}

	\begin{proof}
		(1) and (2) are equivalent by Theorem 5.1 (instead of $\pp = \p$ the equivalent statement $\cp = \p$ \cite{ogi93} is used). (1) implies (3), (4), (5), and (6) because these functions are computable in polynomial time (for (6) it suffices to consider the function $min(plu(a_{1}, ..., a_{k}))$). \\

		Let $L \in \cp$. By Proposition 4.4, there exist a function $f \in \totp$ and a polynomial $p$ such that for any $x \in \Sigma^{*}$:
		\begin{align*}
			x \in L \Leftrightarrow f(x) = 2^{p(|x|)}, \\
			x \notin L \Leftrightarrow f(x) < 2^{p(|x|)}. 
		\end{align*}

		\noindent $(3 \Rightarrow 2)$: Let $g(x) = f(x) \ominus (2^{p(|x|)} - 1)$. By assumption, $g \in \totp$, and for any $x \in \Sigma^{*}$:
		\begin{align*}
			x \in L \Leftrightarrow g(x) = 1, \\
			x \notin L \Leftrightarrow g(x) = 0.
		\end{align*}
		By Proposition 3.2, $g \in \fp$, thus $L \in \p$. \\

		\noindent $(4 \Rightarrow 2)$: Let $g(x) = \lfloor f(x) / 2^{p(|x|)} \rfloor$. By assumption, $g \in \totp$, and for any $x \in \Sigma^{*}$:
		\begin{align*}
			x \in L \Leftrightarrow g(x) = 1, \\
			x \notin L \Leftrightarrow g(x) = 0.
		\end{align*}
		By Proposition 3.2, $g \in \fp$, thus $L \in \p$. \\

		\noindent $(5 \Rightarrow 2)$: Let $g(x) = span(f(x), 2^{p(|x|)})$. By assumption, $g \in \totp$, and for any $x \in \Sigma^{*}$:
		\begin{align*}
			x \in L \Leftrightarrow g(x) = 1, \\
			x \notin L \Leftrightarrow g(x) = 2.
		\end{align*} 
		By Proposition 3.2, $g \in \fp$, thus $L \in \p$. \\

		\noindent $(6 \Rightarrow 2)$: By assumption, there exists a function $g \in \totp$ such that for any $x \in \Sigma^{*}$: $g(x) \in plu(0, 0, 2f(x), f(x) + 2^{p(|x|)}, 2^{p(|x|) + 1})$,
		\begin{align*}
			x &\in L \Leftrightarrow g(x) = 2^{p(|x|) + 1}, \\
			x &\notin L \Leftrightarrow g(x) = 0.
		\end{align*}
		By Proposition 3.3, $L \in \p$. \qed
	\end{proof}



