The essence of the framework is captured by the \textit{framework ontology}, visualised in \Cref{fig:framework}: a collection of sets and functions capturing concepts and their relationships.
We distinguish between \textit{static} and \textit{dynamic} concepts, and discuss each in a dedicated subsection.
The framework is to be \textit{instantiated} by a runtime system that defines the framework's sets and functions such that the \textit{framework requirements} are preserved;
together, \Cref{sec:impl,sec:slick} define such an instantiation.
The most fundamental framework requirements are expressed in the figure; for example, each \textit{action} has exactly one \textit{justification}, and each \textit{agreement} is a \textit{message}.
The remaining framework requirements are introduced throughout this section.
The framework guarantees
\Cref{prop:agents_agree_on_permission,prop:agents_agree_on_effects}, \ie, agents always agree on action \textit{permission} and \textit{effects}, which are both precisely defined in \Cref{sec:framework_dynamics}.

Ultimately, we explain how agents can act autonomously while enforcing the \textit{well-behavedness} (\Cref{def:well-behaved}) of themselves and their peers in practice.

\begin{proppy}
\label{prop:agents_agree_on_permission}
Agents always agree whether a given action is permitted.
\end{proppy}
\begin{proppy}
\label{prop:agents_agree_on_effects}
Agents always agree on the effects of a given action.
\end{proppy}

\begin{deffy}
\label{def:well-behaved}
An agent is \textit{well-behaved} while all their actions are permitted.
\end{deffy}


