\subsection{Statics}
\label{sec:framework_statics}
The framework statics are fixed at runtime.
They concern the fundamentals of the framework: data types and the syntax and semantics of the policy language.
Agents reason about structure and relationship between given statics without having to communicate.

The \textbf{agents}\footnote{
    Unless otherwise specified, let framework agents coincide with agents of the underlying distributed system.
} of the framework author \textbf{messages} occurring dynamically as statements, \ie, the messages are fixed statically, and some subset of messages are \textit{stated} per runtime state.
Statements communicate \textbf{policies} (see \textbf{extract} below).
We take for granted that $(\in)$ and \textit{singleton} relate messages to \textit{message sets} as expected.
The \textbf{truths} define a relation over facts and policies, whose membership we test with $\mathit{true}(f,p) \triangleq \exists t \in \mathit{truths}:$ $\mathit{of}(t)=f \wedge \mathit{in}(t)=p$.
Alternatively, each policy prescribes which facts are true.
%
In the examples throughout this section, we instantiate policies as the subsets of the \textit{(policy) rules} shown in \Cref{tab:eg_rules}.
Hence, $(\cup)$ is a natural policy-composition operator, uniting rules.
But note that uniting rules does not generally unite truths.
We use the semantics of \dlneg, as it is informally explained in \Cref{sec:dlneg}, to define facts and truth: $f$ is true in $p$ iff $f \in [p]$, the \textit{stable model} of policy~$p$, \eg, when $f=\text{\lstinline{error}}$ and $p=\{r_m\}$.
Finally, \textbf{effects} are are special subset of truths that each identifies its \textbf{affector} agent.
In \Cref{sec:framework_dynamics}, we explain how effects are the foundation for the interface between the runtime system and the outside world.
% clarify the role of effects in interfacing actions with the world outside the runtime system.
% Intuitively, effects are truths
% In \Cref{def:enacted_effect_of}, we describe the utility of effects in relation to actions.
% Intuitively,
% Effects are intended for use as ``hooks'' for behaviour outside of the runtime system.
% Agents are motivated to cause or prevent effects directly, and only indirectly motivated to influence statements and actions for their ultimate relation to effects. 
In our example instantiation, we let (only) truths of \lstinline|ctl-deletes(bob,data1)| be effects, affected by Bob.


The definition of \textbf{valid} fixes the distinction between useful and useless policies, as a function of their contents.
% In summary, validity determines which actions are permitted, by fixing the distinction between valid and invalid policies.
We instantiate \textbf{valid} to characterise policies without errors; precisely, we let $p \in \mathit{valid} \triangleq \neg\mathit{true}(\text{\lstinline{error}}, p)$.
Despite the simplicity of this definition, (in)validity emerges from complex rule interactions, which is key to policies capturing the complex relationships between domain concepts.
As a simple example, no valid policy is a superset of $\{r_m, r_n\}$, as these rules suffice to ensure that \lstinline{error} is tautological, \ie, true in any case.
Intuitively, $\{r_m, r_n\}$ captures the requirement that there a must be a single consistent answer to the question: must Amy confirm?
\Cref{sec:brane_control_plane} demonstrates how rules conditioning (in)validity lay the groundwork for complex inter-agent power dynamics.

Most directly, \textbf{extract} relates messages to their policy contents.
However, it also determines which policies agents are capable of expressing.
For example, agent~$a$ simply cannot express~$p$ if \textit{extract} maps no statements to~$p$.
Intuitively, \textit{extract} can ensure that the extracted policy reflects its author(s).
Hence, we understand the policy~$\mathit{extract}(m)$ as the subjective assertions of agent~$\mathit{author}(m)$.
For our running example with \dlneg, we instantiate \textit{extract} as a pure function of the message author and the message \textit{payload}, which is an arbitrary policy, chosen by the author. 
Intuitively, \textit{extract} invalidates payloads on the condition that they include any undesirable author-rule pairs.
\hypertarget{inst_ownership}{Precisely, we let $\mathit{extract}(\{m\}) \triangleq \mathit{payload}(m) \cup \{ \text{\lstinline{error}} \mid \forall (r,a)\in\mathit{owns}: (r\in{}m \wedge \neg\mathit{author}(m,a)) \}$, where \textit{owns} relates rule~$r$ to agent~$a$ if $r$ has an antecedent whose predicate is prefixed by \lstinline{ctl-} and whose first parameter is constant~$a$.}
For example, only Bob owns rule \lstinline{ctl-deletes(bob, data1)}, while rule \lstinline{error :- confirms(amy)} has no owner because fact \lstinline{error} has neither prefix \lstinline{ctrl-} nor a constant first parameter.
