Here we break down and explain \Cref{def:permitted}: whether a given action is permitted, in the context of a fixed (dynamic) system configuration.
Firstly, justifications must consist entirely of \textbf{stated} messages.
This ensures that justifications reflect the existing assertions of their respective authors, \ie, and not arbitrary policies in conceivable messages.
Hence, agents restrict actions by withholding their statements.
For example, Amy cannot justify actions in terms of Bob's hypothetically-stated messages; Bob must state them first!
Secondly, justifications must be \textbf{based}, \ie, include their basis agreement.
Thus, the meaning of a justification is regulated by its basis.
For example, \lstinline{ctl-trusts(amy, bob)} is necessarily true in all justifications based on $\{r_t\}$.
% Thus, the current agreements determines the justifiable actions.
% In \Cref{sec:brane_control_plane}, we demonstrate how this kind of reasoning underpins the design process of the initial agreement for the case study system.
Thirdly, (the policy extracted from) each justification must be \textbf{valid}.
These justifications have internally-consistent meanings.
For example, recall that only Bob owns~$r_d$.
Hence, Amy can make statements whose payloads include $r_d$, but those statements can be safely ignored, as they are useless in justifying permitted actions.
% can never be included in valid justifications.
% As \textit{extract} restricts statements with valid policies as a function of their author, some useful policies require the cooperation of several agents.
% Hence, without Bob's participation, Amy's actions cannot be justified
% Thus, working alone, Amy has limited statements, hence limited justifications, hence limited permitted actions.
%
% These conditions comprise the definition of \textit{permitted} which is preserved by changes at runtime;
% actors can rest assured that their justified actions are necessarily justified in future.
In general, definitions of validity are useful if they enable meaningful reasoning about policies in terms of their parts.
For example, for any conceivable policy~p, the composite policy $\{r_m, r_n\} \cup p$ is invalid, because $r_m$ and $r_n$ place contradictory conditions on validity: Amy cannot both confirm and not confirm!
Finally, actions must be \textbf{current}: based on a current time.
This is the only component of permission that may be falsified in the future, \ie, all the other components that hold at present necessarily hold in the future.
In general, we expect agents to reason about historical and hypothetical system configurations; they come to reliable conclusions.
For example, Amy and Bob agree to change the current time from 1 to 2.
Actions permitted at time~1 are unaffected, but they are no longer permitted at the current time.
Instead, new actions are permitted, based on the agreement~$\{\}$ at time~2.
\Cref{sec:brane_control_plane} lays out a realistically complex agreement and discusses its underlying intuition by reasoning about the actions it permits.
Thereafter, \Cref{sec:scenario:dynamic_agreement} demonstrates changing this agreement by adding a changed agreement and changing the current time. 

