\subsection{Design Considerations}
\label{sec:impl_design_considerations}

Data exchange systems are subject to many design considerations before they can be implemented in detail.
Here, we explore particular design considerations, and explain the decisions reflected in our prototype implementations.

Firstly, \textbf{how is the (meta) data managed}?
Our prototype system centralises (meta) data stores within shared memory.
This simplifies the data exchange implementation itself, such that our article can focus on the main concern of our contribution: (regulating) the interactions between the agents.
For example, it trivialises the (de)serialisation of inter-agent communications, and the collection of diagnostic information during experiments, \eg, we can easily inspect ensire system snapshots to compare the views of different agents.
Realistic data exchange system implementations would instead require physically distributed stores of (meta) data, which requires further design decisions (\eg, how are agreements synchronised?) which we leave out of scope.
In anticipation of future experiments focused on these concerns, our implementation preserves a faithful abstraction of a distributed system.
For example, an agent cannot observe a statement it has not created or received from a peer.

Secondly, \textbf{how is well-behavedness defined}?
Our implementation adopts the view of the JustAct framework: well-behaved agents take only permitted actions, where permission is regulated by agents' policies.
We postpone the rest of the discussion until \Cref{sec:slick}, where we define and use the \slick language in particular, and characterise its policies.

Finally, \textbf{how is well-behavedness enforced}?
As a design tenet, the JustAct framework \textit{defines} permission and well-behavedness orthogonally to their means of enforcement;
each agent necessarily has sufficient information to check if any observed action is permitted.
However, different enforcement mechanisms affect agent incentives.
When misbehaviour is \textit{prevented} (ex-ante enforcement), agents can reason in absolutes about (im)possibility via (im)permissibility, \eg, affording their assessment of the risks of harm incurred by misbehaviour. 
When misbehaviour is \textit{punished} (ex-post enforcement), it has consequences for agents' behaviour in the future.
We leave these considerations out of scope of this article, and implement an enforcement mechanism that suffices to support our experiments and emphasise the unified view of permission:
the prototype data exchange implementation lets agents behave arbitrarily, but logs this behaviour to enable perfect (external) audits after the fact.
The trace suffices to recognise and diagnose all misbehaviour.
Our prototype is provided with a supplementary tool for performing and visualising these diagnoses.
An example of the visualisation is provided in \Cref{fig:prototype-tui}.
The details of enforcement in the data- and control-planes are discussed in \Cref{sec:impl_data_plane,sec:impl_control_plane}, respectively.

