\subsection{The Data Plane}
\label{sec:impl_data_plane}

Agents are ultimately motivated to interact with the data plane, where their data is stored and shared.
By our shared-memory implementation, the sharing of data between agents is trivial; each agent has a view on the same data store.


Agents' \textit{read} or \textit{write} (together:~\textit{access}) data in the shared data store.
The runtime system regulates these events by requiring that each realises an \textit{enacted effect} in the control plane.
We define \textit{(enacted) effects} precisely in \Cref{sec:slick_to_impl_effects} atop the policy language.
But here, it suffices to say that agents must contextualise each of their access events by identifying the corresponding action (explicitly), data (explicitly), and affector (implicitly:~themselves).
Agents are able to perform any access events, but the runtime system logs this behaviour along with its context.
The runtime system realises the intended correspondence between access events and enacted effects.
After the fact, auditors are able to determine precisely which access events \textit{realise} enacted effects.
\Cref{sec:impl_control_plane} follows by detailing the related concerns of the control plane, including the creation and permission of actions. 




