\subsubsection{Controlled Gossip of Stated Messages}
\label{sec:impl_statement_gossip}

The statics relating to messages are implemented as data types and (field) projection functions.
Each message is a product of a \textit{message identifier} and a \textit{payload}: an arbitrary policy.
Each message identifier is a pair of its author and a descriminator: arbitrary facts.
In the definition of $\mathit{author'}$ is the first of many times we use what we call the ``prefix trick'' to ensure there exists a functional projection of type $X \rightarrow Y$; we let $X \triangleq Y \times Z$ for some discriminator $Z$, and project $X$ to its first element.
\begin{deffy}[Message Identifiers]
\label{def:msg_id}
$\mathit{messageIds} \triangleq \mathit{facts} \times \mathit{facts}$.
\end{deffy}
\begin{deffy}[Messages]
$\mathit{messages} \triangleq \mathit{messageIds} \times \mathit{policies}$.
\end{deffy}
\begin{deffy}[Payloads]
$\mathit{payload}(\langle i, p \rangle : \mathit{messages}) \triangleq p$.
\end{deffy}
\begin{deffy}[Author]
$\mathit{author}(\langle i, p \rangle : \mathit{messages}) \triangleq \mathit{author'}(i)$
$$\text{\normalfont where } \mathit{author'}(\langle f, f' \rangle : \mathit{messageIds}) \triangleq f.$$
\end{deffy}

The dynamic creation and communication of statements is implemented as a store of stated messages.
Conceptually, we reason about the messages that have been \textit{sent} by which sender.
Thus we can precisely characterise \textit{gossip}, where agents can repeatedly send the same messages (\eg, to new recipients).
In real distributed systems, gossip is a flexible and scalable means of disseminating non-critical information.
Crucially, the sender of a message is distinct from its \textit{author}, which is evident in the message itself.
\begin{deffy}[Sent Messages]
$\mathit{sent} \triangleq \mathit{agent} \times \mathit{messages}$.
\end{deffy}
\begin{deffy}[Statements]
$\mathit{statements} \triangleq \{ m \mid \forall \langle a, m \rangle \in \mathit{sent} \}$.
\end{deffy}

The runtime controls the gossip of statements to preserve their intended interpretations.
\Cref{prop:message_provenance} ensures that all sent messages are (at least) sent by their authors.
Hence, gossiped statements necessarily originates at their authors, and thus, express the authors' subjective assertions.
\Cref{prop:identifiable_payloads} identifies payloads via message identifiers.
This lets agents define policies that refer to (other) policies.
For example, near the end of \Cref{sec:init_impl_for_brane}, \hyperlink{relying_on_payload_id}{\textit{authorisations} are made applicable only if only (payloads of) the given messages define the given \textit{tasks}}.
The preservation of both properties is simple in the implementation, because the view of each agent is managed by the runtime system; it suffices to check which messages the sender has previously received (for \Cref{prop:message_provenance}) and sent (for \Cref{prop:identifiable_payloads}).
\begin{proppy}[Message Provenance]
\label{prop:message_provenance}
$\forall \langle a, m \rangle \in \mathit{sent}, \langle \mathit{author}(m), m \rangle \in \mathit{sent}$.
\end{proppy}
\begin{proppy}[Identifiable Payloads]
\label{prop:identifiable_payloads}
$\forall \langle i, p \rangle, \langle i', p' \rangle  \in \mathit{statements}, i = i' \rightarrow p = p'$.
\end{proppy}


\subsubsection{Controlled Gossip of Taken Actions}
The JustAct concept of \textbf{action} is implemented similarly to that of statements.
The static component of actions are a composite data type, the composition of their actors, justifications, and basis agreements.
The dynamic component concerns the gossip of (taken) actions.
As with statements, agents' requests to act are regulated to preserve provenance;
agents can gossip about other agents' actions, but agents cannot \textit{create} new actions on another actor's behalf.

Unlike statements, the creation of new actions is regulated.
The runtime system enforces that only \textit{permitted} actions are taken:
after the fact, an auditor is able to evaluate the meta data in the system trace, and check which actions were permitted when they were created.
\Cref{fig:prototype-tui} demonstrates such an audit, confirming that the action under inspection indeed satisfies all four conditions of permission~(see \Cref{def:permitted}).



