\section{The \slick Policy Language \& Interpreter}
\label{sec:slick}

We use \textit{policy language} to refer to a set of definitions of JustAct concepts concerning policies.
%
The definition of \lstinline{policy} captures the syntax of the language.
%
The definitions of \lstinline{truth} (via \lstinline{fact}, \lstinline{in}, and \lstinline{of}) and \lstinline{valid} capture orthogonal semantic notions of policies.
Hence, \Cref{sec:impl,sec:slick} together complete a particular instantiation of the JustAct framework.

\subsection{Design Considerations}

The framework imposes several essential requirements on the policy language.
The most essential requirement is expressed in \Cref{fig:framework} itself: 
truth must be a static relation over facts and policies.
In practice, this means that the truth of a given fact in a given policy must be computable via a deterministic and terminating procedure.
This requirement underpins the purpose of the same policies to be consistently understood by different agents, or at different times.
For example, (complete) Prolog is an unsuitable policy language, because its inference procedure is non-terminating.
Even if agents agree on a Prolog program, their conclusions are not consistently reproduced.
Consider a case where Amy justifies the processing of Bob's medical data with a Prolog policy which Amy successfully validated.
Three hours later, an auditor checks the justification, but their evaluation of the Prolog policy times out.
The auditor cannot distinguish between an invalid policy and a policy that Amy intentionally crafted to time out.
Should Amy be punished for acting?
In such an environment, would Amy risk acting in the first place?

\subsection{The \slick Language}
\label{sec:slick_lang}

For our experiments, we use a bespoke policy language that we refer to as \textit{\slick}.
It is based on \dlneg, so its fundamentals remain similar: each program consists of a sequence of rules (but whose ordering is irrelevant to the semantics) whose antecedents are possibly negated.
For each policy, the semantics prescribes Boolean truth to a finite subset of the infinite syntactic category of ground (\ie, variable-free) facts.
We complete the description of \slick by enumerating its differences from \dlneg;
observe that each difference is either superficial or based on well-established research:
\begin{enumerate}
\item
    \slick departs from the traditional \textbf{concrete syntax} of \dlneg to (in our opinion) aid in the readability of long rules.
    We conjoin atoms with \lstinline{and} and arguments of atoms with white space (both instead of (\lstinline{,})) and we separate consequents from antecedents by \lstinline{if} instead of~(\lstinline{:-}).
    The use of \lstinline{not} is unchanged, as it already suits the new style.

    \item 
    \textbf{Inference} uses the operational form \cite{przymusinski_well-founded_1990} of the \textit{well-founded semantics for general logic programming with negation}~\cite{van_gelder_well-founded_1991};
    a value in $\{\textit{true}, \textit{false}, \textit{unknown}\}$ is deterministically assigned to every conceivable policy.
    Notably, the semantics robustly preserves meaning even in cases that many alternative semantics (\eg, the stable model semantics) collapses.
    Also notably, the majority of cases (\eg, all policies examined in this article), the two semantics coincide anyway.
    For the purposes of JustAct, it suffices to conflate \textit{unknown} and \textit{false} valuations of facts: neither is true.
    For example, \lstinline{p if not p} formalises a logical contradiction, giving \lstinline{p} unknown value; agents agree that~\lstinline{p} is not true.

\item \label{item:recursive-atoms}
    \textbf{Facts} are inductively defined.
    As in \dlneg, facts may be constructed by applying a constant predicate symbol to a list of constants or variables.
    However, unlike \dlneg, (sub)facts may occur wherever variables may occur, and variables quantify over \textit{ground facts}, and not only constant symbols.
    In database and logic programming literature, this feature is well-studied, and is usually called \textit{function symbols}~\cite{DBLP:books/sp/Lloyd84,DBLP:journals/jlp/RamakrishnanU95,DBLP:conf/sebd/CalauttiGMT15,DBLP:journals/tplp/GrecoMT13}.
    The syntax of rules are generalised accordingly; consequents and antecedents are facts that are possibly composed from smaller facts.
    Variables may occur in facts at any depth.
    Naturally, the semantics is generalised to handle these composite facts.
    This generalisation lets \slick policies more easily model various other forms of policy, for example, policies expressed in eFLINT via nested eFLINT fact-instances.
    In general, this feature affords the more terse and intuitive definition of policies which are comparatively cumbersome and un-intuitive when expressed in \dlneg.
    For example, consider how the \slick rule \lstinline{some vote if Agent votes} is applicable regardless of the internal structure of the fact binding the variable \lstinline{Agent}, which would otherwise require a unique \dlneg rule specialised to each shape of \dlneg fact to be bound by \lstinline{Agent}.

\item \label{item:slick-flat-atoms}
    \textbf{Fact arguments are homogenous} and parenthesis are only needed to avoid structural ambiguity. 
    For example, \lstinline[language=prolog]{eats(amy,apples)} in \dlneg can be translated into \slick as \lstinline{amy eats apples}, which is read very naturally.
    Firstly, because predicates are constants like any other, they can be abstracted by variables; consider how, in the prior example, only in \slick could constant \lstinline{eats} be abstracted, \eg, by variable \lstinline{Uses}.
    Secondly, note how this reduces the visual complexity of rules, because one layer of parenthesis is avoided.

\item 
    \slick supports \textbf{(in)equality constraints} over (all pairs in sets of) facts as antecedents, using keywords \lstinline{same} and \lstinline{diff}.
    This is a well-studied feature of logic programming languages, \eg, described in \cite{DBLP:journals/jlp/GoguenM84}.
    In practice, it can tersely capture common patterns in agreements, where the applicability of key rules is conditioned on particular facts (mis)matching.
    For example, \lstinline|error if X authorises and not same { X amy }| is applicable if \lstinline{X} binds \lstinline{bob}, but not if \lstinline{X} binds~\lstinline{amy}.
    Note \lstinline|not same { X Y Z }| and \lstinline|diff { X Y Z }| have different meanings; only the former is satisfied in the case where $\text{\lstinline{X}}=\text{\lstinline{Y}}\neq\text{\lstinline{Z}}$.

\item
    As we have described it thus far (``ideal \slick''), the inference procedure underlying the \slick semantics is non-terminating.
    For example, consider attempting to finish enumerating the truths of \lstinline{Fact is true if Fact. it is raining}.
    Unfortunately, ideal \slick belongs to the large subset of conceivable logic programming languages for which checking termination is undecidable.
    The space of workarounds and compromises has been thoroughly researched for many years~\cite{DBLP:journals/jlp/SchreyeD94}.
    As this is not the focus of this work, we opt for a pragmatic approach to ensuring that inference terminates: we \textbf{bound the maximum depth of true facts} to a statically-configured parameter, as in \cite{chomicki1995depth}.
    However, where \cite{chomicki1995depth} ignores facts deeper than the bound, we instead detect and make explicit when the bound is exceeded: each such policy is trivialised and invalidated; precisely, its denotation is replaced by that of the policy \lstinline{error. bound exceeded.}
    Thus, for all policies,
    \begin{enumerate*}
        \item with enough computational resources\footnote{\slick affords additional constraints to further restrict valid policies to the benefit of bounding the resources needed for reasoning. For example, one can limit the number of truths before the policy is invalidated.}, queries are eventually answered, and
        \item validity guarantees that each logical implication encoded by each rule is satisfied by its truths.
    \end{enumerate*}

\item
    We define validity as for \dlneg in \Cref{sec:framework_statics}: policy~$p$ is valid unless the fact~\lstinline{error} is true in~$p$.
    Other than this special meaning, \lstinline{error} is an entirely ordinary fact.
    \Cref{sec:scenarios} demonstrates, and \Cref{sec:discussion} discusses, how \slick affords agents' \textit{cooperative specification} of shared policies, via its \textit{composition control} language features~\cite{esterhuyse2024cooperative}.
    In summary, agents agree that only \textit{valid} justifications are useful, where validity captures requirements of the contributors to the policy, including requirements on the runtime context of the contributions themselves.
    Note that, by using features \ref{item:recursive-atoms} and \ref{item:slick-flat-atoms}, we can introduce a degree of explainability by deriving verbose~\lstinline{error}-facts that refer to the rule deriving them; e.g., the program~\lstinline{error (b was true) if b. error if error X.} will implement validity as expected while the model of the program provides additional information why it is invalid.
\end{enumerate}



