\subsection{Connecting Policies to the Runtime System}
We complete the instantiation of the JustAct framework by defining the remaining concepts in \Cref{fig:framework}.
These final concepts inter-connect the policy language (in \Cref{sec:slick}, currently) with the rest of the runtime system (in \Cref{sec:impl}, previously).

\subsubsection{Defining Effects and Affectors}
\label{sec:slick_to_impl_effects}
Effects connect the (output of) the policy reasoning process to the behaviour of agents in the data exchange system: agents realise the effects of (justifications of) actions as the reading and writing of data in the data plane.

Recall from \Cref{fig:framework} that effects themselves are a static concept: they are a subset of \textit{truths} that statically identify their \textit{affector} agents.
We let each truth of a fact matching \lstinline|Agent reads Data| or \lstinline|Agent writes Data| be effects, and let their affectors be~\lstinline{Agent}.
Hence, without any runtime context, it is evident that the policy \lstinline{amy reads data1. amy is happy.} has two truths, but only the effect \lstinline{amy reads data1}, whose affector is~\lstinline{amy}.
An effect is connected to a particular action, at runtime, when the effect is \textit{enacted} (\Cref{def:enacted_effect_of}).
Agents can \textit{realise} these effects by influencing the data plane in the corresponding manner.
For example, once the effect \lstinline{amy reads data1} is enacted, it can be realised by Amy reading the data identified by~\lstinline{data1}.

Because effects are the interface between the control- and data-plane, any definition of effects ultimately determines the granularity at which policies can regulate the data plane.
By our definition, each effect identifies its affector and the data, but notably, it does not identify the \textit{contents} of the data.
Consequently, in this article, policies cannot distinguish the contents written or read by agents in the data plane.


\subsubsection{Reflecting Policy Context via Extract}
\label{sec:impl_to_slick_extract}

Finally, $\mathit{message~set}\funarrow{extract}\mathit{policy}$ yields the policy contents of any given (set of) messages: the input to the policy reasoning process.
As was done in \dlneg in \Cref{sec:framework_statics}, our definition is such that the policy extracted from an individual message~\lstinline{Msg} reflects its runtime context.
This is implemented in Rust as a function with the type signature \lstinline[language=rust]{fn extract_from(payload: Policy, msg_id: GroundAtom) -> Policy}.
Precisely, the result is a syntactic transformation of the arbitrary \textit{payload}:
for each rule, each existing consequent \lstinline{Fact} is supplemented by the consequent \lstinline{Fact within Msg}.
Intuitively, messages can assert any truths, but not without tracing back to the message identifier (and the author).
As in \Cref{sec:framework_statics}, \textit{extract} is applicable to non-singleton message sets also, in which case the result is the composition of the policies extracted from each message independently.
Finally, in the context of (the justification of) an action by actor~\lstinline{Agent}, the extracted policy undergoes a second syntactic transformation: the rule \lstinline{actor Agent} is added.

Agents rely on \textit{extract} to reflect the runtime context of a message in (a transformed version of) its payload.
Notably, truths of the form \lstinline{Fact within (Author M)} trace the truth of \lstinline{Fact} to the message \lstinline{Author M} authored by \lstinline{Author}.
In \Cref{sec:case_study}, to follow, these features let the usual rules of \slick specify the powers of agents to make statements and take actions, or to (conditionally) \textit{delegate} these powers to other agents.
But for now, we demonstrate with a more familiar example:
the \slick rules below encode the \hyperlink{inst_ownership}{\textit{ownership} mechanism}, defined in natural language in \Cref{sec:framework_statics}, regulating the authorship of the rules above.
Consequently, for example, rules asserting \lstinline{amy confirms} are invalid unless they are authored by \lstinline{amy}.
\begin{lstlisting}
error if      (amy confirms) within (Author M) and diff { Author amy }.
error if      (amy trusts X) within (Author M) and diff { Author bob }.
error if (bob deletes data1) within (Author M) and diff { Author bob }.
\end{lstlisting}

