The final part of the initial agreement encodes the roles of checkers in \brane.
In summary, the execution of planned workflow tasks is conditioned on the authorisation of all \textit{involved} agents.
% Note how this encoding reuses the restriction on \lstinline{says} to distinguish different agents' authorisations.
% Later we demonstrate that this does not prevent agents from delegating power over their authorisations to their peers intentionally.
We reproduce \brane's inductive definition of involvement from \cite{KASSEM2024107550}.
In the base case, agents are involved with data they control.
In the inductive case, a checker's involvement propagates over tasks from their outputs to their inputs, in the direction of data flow.
Intuitively, checkers are involved with all derivatives of the data under their control.
This mechanism gives each involved checker the power to veto the task's execution by withholding their authorisation, \eg, reflecting their policy decisions without otherwise revealing the domain policies themselves~\cite{DBLP:conf/eScience/EsterhuyseMBB22}.
For example, a hospital authorises a particular trusted worker to process their sensitive medical records, but vetos the result being further processed elsewhere by withholding any further authorisations. 

\hypertarget{relying_on_payload_id}{The final rule} protects authorisers from unintentionally authorising tasks with unexpected inputs.
Precisely, agents have a means of asserting that the given message \textit{closes the inputs} (\Cref{def:input_closed}) of some task~$t$;
each authorisation is scoped to a particular message, and the policy is invalidated if any other message attributes input variables to the authorised task.
Consider how drivers cannot be prevented from defining $t$ differently in different statements.
However, this rule prevents workers from executing $t$ with some input~$v$ (from one message) as justified by an authorisation without~$v$ (from another message), unintentionally or otherwise.



\begin{deffy}[Messages~$M$ close the inputs of task~$t$]
\label{def:input_closed}
$\mathit{closeInputs(M, t)} \  \triangleq$
\begin{align*}
\forall v \in \mathit{facts}, M' \supseteq M, \quad & \neg\mathit{true}(\mathit{extract}(M\phantom{'}), \ t \ \text{\lstinline|has input|} \ v) \ \wedge \
\\
& \phantom{\neg}\mathit{true}(\mathit{extract}(M'), \ t \ \text{\lstinline|has input|} \ v)
\quad  \rightarrow \quad  \neg\mathit{valid}(\mathit{extract}(M')).
\end{align*}
\end{deffy}


% All together, this mechanism gives agents the power to veto the execution of any task~$t$ that processed (derivatives of) their data with undesirable (combinations of) inputs or workers.
% For example, a hospital authorises the processing of their sensitive medical records by another trusted hospital, but vetos the result being further processed by a third party.


