Now, let us form discrete-time of the function in eq. (\ref{eq:h_ij(t)}) w.r.t global time window as
\begin{equation}
	h_{ijk}[n] = 
	\begin{cases} 
		1 & \text{if }  n \in [t_m, t_m + \Delta t_m] \cap [t_\text{start}^i, t_\text{end}^i], \\
		0 & \text{otherwise}.
	\end{cases}
	\label{eq:h_ij[n]}
\end{equation}
In fact, eq. (\ref{eq:h_ij[n]}) is a binary sequence where each bit represents one second of information in temporal spectrum, meaning that for a given time instance $t = t_0$, the binary value is 1 if the satellite $x_k$ is in line of sight with ground station $g_i$ in constellation $j$, and 0 otherwise. It is worth noting that one can tune the precision of sampling by $\alpha \ge 1$ seconds by $
h_{ijk}[m] = h_{ijk}[\alpha \cdot m]$ subject to meeting nyquist conditions, yet here we assume none-sampled signal for the sake of simplicity.   
