As we can see from this code snippet, the functionality of the method \emph{"buildId"} is to generate a unique ID for a service object and return it. As a result, developers prefer the variable name 'id' in this context. For this case, \emph{VarNamer} removed the verb \emph{"build"} and recommended \emph{"id"} as the variable name, aligning better with the developer preferences.
However, \emph{Eclipse} recommended \emph{"buildId"} and \emph{IDEA} recommended \emph{"s"}, both of which are sub-optimal.
Additionally, \emph{VarNamer} excels in cases where initializations involve accessing elements from a collection. For example, for initialization \emph{"features.next()"}~\cite{RefactoringExample3}, the involved method declaration is presented in Listing~\ref{code3}. As we can see from this code snippet, \emph{"next"} is a method of \emph{"FeatureIterator"}, and it returns an element from a collection. Consequently, \emph{"feature"}, which is the singular form of \emph{"features"}, is preferred by developers.  With an exploration of real-world refactoring data, \emph{VarNamer} correctly identified \emph{"feature"} as the preferred variable name. However, \emph{Eclipse} and \emph{IDEA} both recommended \emph{"next"} as the variable name, which is sub-optimal.
