\subsubsection{DC OPF Formulation}

The DC OPF for region \( r \) is:
\begin{subequations}\label{dcopf}
		\begin{align}
			&\min _{\boldsymbol{x}^r} \sum_{i \in \Omega^r_g} f(p^g_i) \label{key1}\\	
			&\quad\text { s.t. }\nonumber\\
			& \qquad\quad \underline{p}_{i}^{g}\leqslant p^g_i \leqslant \bar{p}_{i}^{g}, \quad \forall i \in\Omega^r_g \label{key2}\\
			&\qquad\quad p^g_i-p^d_i=\sum_{j \in \Omega^r_i} \frac{\theta_i-\theta_j}{x_{i j}}+\sum_{j \in \Omega^{r,\textrm{in}}_i} \frac{\theta_i-\tilde{\theta}_j}{x_{i j}}, \quad \forall i \in\Omega^r_b \label{key3}\\
			& \qquad\quad \underline{p}_{ij}\leqslant p_{ij} \leqslant \bar{p}_{ij}, \quad \forall ij \in\Omega^r_l \cup \Omega^{r,\textrm{in}}_l \label{key31}\\
			&\qquad\quad\theta_{\text {ref }}=0, \label{key16}\\
			&\qquad\quad \theta_i=\tilde{\theta}_i,\quad \tilde{\theta}_j=\theta_j,  \quad \forall  ij\in\Omega^{r,\textrm{in}}_l \label{key17}
		\end{align}
	\end{subequations}
\( \boldsymbol{x}^r = [\boldsymbol{p}^{g\top}, \boldsymbol{\theta}^\top] \in \mathbb{R}^{n^r_g + n^r_b} \) is the decision vector for region \( r \), including generation \( p^g_i \) and voltage angles \( \theta_i \). Equation~\eqref{key1} minimizes the local cost \( f(p^g_i) \). Constraints~\eqref{key2}--\eqref{key3} enforce generator limits and power balance using demand \( p^d_i \), line reactance \( x_{ij} \), and virtual angles \( \tilde{\theta}_j \). Constraint~\eqref{key31} bounds internal and tie-line flows \( p_{ij} \), and~\eqref{key17} ensures consistency between real and virtual bus angles.


\subsubsection{AC OPF Formulation}

The AC OPF for region \( r \) is:
\begin{subequations}\label{acopf}
    \begin{align}
			&\min _{\boldsymbol{x}^r} \sum_{i \in \Omega^r_g} f(p^g_i) \label{acopf1}\\	
			&\quad\text{s.t.} \nonumber\\
			&\qquad \underline{p}_{i}^{g} \leqslant p^g_i \leqslant \bar{p}_{i}^{g}, \quad \underline{q}_{i}^{g} \leqslant q^g_i \leqslant \bar{q}_{i}^{g}, \quad \forall i \in \Omega^r_g \label{acopf2}\\
			&\qquad p^g_i - p^d_i = \sum_{j \in \Omega^r_i}\left(p_{ij,f}+p_{ij,t}\right)  + \sum_{j \in \Omega^{r,\text{in}}_i} \tilde{p}_{ij}, \quad \forall i \in \Omega^r_b \label{acopf4}\\
			&\qquad q^g_i - q^d_i = \sum_{j \in \Omega^r_i} \left(q_{ij,f}+q_{ij,t}\right) + \sum_{j \in \Omega^{r,\text{in}}_i} \tilde{q}_{ij}, \quad \forall i \in \Omega^r_b \label{acopf5}\\
            &\qquad  p_{ij,f} = G_{ij,1}v_i^2 + G_{ij,2}v_iv_j\cos(\theta_i - \theta_j) \nonumber\\&\qquad\qquad\quad+ B_{ij,2}v_iv_j\sin(\theta_i - \theta_j), \quad \forall ij \in \Omega^r_l \label{acopf6'}\\
            &\qquad q_{ij,f} = -B_{ij,1}v_i^2 - B_{ij,2}v_iv_j\cos(\theta_i - \theta_j) \nonumber\\&\qquad\qquad\quad+ G_{ij,2}v_iv_j\sin(\theta_i - \theta_j), \quad \forall ij \in \Omega^r_l \label{acopf7'}   \\ 
            &\qquad p_{ij,t} = G_{ij,4}v_j^2 + G_{ij,3}v_iv_j\cos(\theta_j - \theta_i) \nonumber\\&\qquad\qquad\quad+ B_{ij,3}v_iv_j\sin(\theta_j - \theta_i), \quad \forall ij \in \Omega^r_l \label{acopf8'}\\
            &\qquad q_{ij,t} = -B_{ij,4}v_j^2 - B_{ij,3}v_iv_j\cos(\theta_j - \theta_i)\nonumber\\&\qquad\qquad\quad + G_{ij,3}v_iv_j\sin(\theta_j - \theta_i), \quad \forall ij \in \Omega^r_l \label{acopf9'}\\
            & \qquad p_{ij,f}^2 + q_{ij,f}^2 \leq \bar{p}_{ij}^2 + \bar{q}_{ij}^2, \quad \forall ij \in \Omega^r_l \cup \Omega^{r,\textrm{in}}_l \label{acopf10'}\\
           &\qquad  p_{ij,t}^2 + q_{ij,t}^2 \leq \bar{p}_{ij}^2 + \bar{q}_{ij}^2, \quad \forall ij \in \Omega^r_l \cup \Omega^{r,\textrm{in}}_l \label{acopf11'}\\
			&\qquad \underline{\Delta\theta}_{ij} \leq \theta_i - \theta_j \leq \overline{\Delta\theta}_{ij}, \quad \forall ij \in \Omega^r_l\cup \Omega^{r,\textrm{in}}_l \label{acopf12}\\
			&\qquad \theta_{\text{ref}} = 0 \label{acopf14}\\
        &\qquad \theta_i=\tilde{\theta}_i,\quad \tilde{\theta}_j=\theta_j,  \quad \forall  ij\in\Omega^{r,\textrm{in}}_l \label{acopf15}  \\
        &\qquad v_i=\tilde{v}_i,\quad \tilde{v}_j=v_j,  \quad \forall  ij\in\Omega^{r,\textrm{in}}_l \label{acopf16} 
		\end{align}
\end{subequations}
The decision vector is \( \boldsymbol{x}^r = [\boldsymbol{p}^{g\top}, \boldsymbol{q}^{g\top}, \boldsymbol{\theta}^\top, \boldsymbol{v}^\top] \), including generation \( (p^g_i, q^g_i) \), angles \( \theta_i \), and voltages \( v_i \) at region \( r \). Constraints~\eqref{acopf2}--\eqref{acopf5} enforce generator and nodal power limits;~\eqref{acopf6'}--\eqref{acopf9'} define AC flows using admittance terms \( G_{ij,k}, B_{ij,k} \). Limits on flows and angle differences are imposed in~\eqref{acopf10'}--\eqref{acopf12}. Reference angle~\eqref{acopf14} and virtual bus equalities~\eqref{acopf15}--\eqref{acopf16} ensure boundary consistency.


\subsubsection{ADMM Coordination}

To ensure consistency between neighboring regions, we use ADMM to coordinate virtual variables \cite{erseghe2014distributed}. For example, the voltage angle consistency is enforced via:
\begin{equation}
L_\rho(\theta, \tilde{\theta}, \lambda) = f(p^g) + \lambda^\top (\theta - \tilde{\theta}) + \frac{\rho}{2} \|\theta - \tilde{\theta}\|^2,
\end{equation}
where \( \lambda \) are the dual variables and \( \rho \) is the penalty parameter. Similar terms are added for voltage magnitudes in AC OPF.

Each ADMM iteration proceeds as:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Solve each region’s local OPF using the current dual variables.
    \item Update dual variables based on tie-line consistency.
    \item Check primal residuals and stop if below a threshold.
\end{enumerate}


In general, coordination across regions is achieved using the ADMM. The distributed DC OPF and AC OPF formulations in~\eqref{dcopf} and~\eqref{acopf} incorporate local objectives and tie-line consistency via augmented Lagrangian. Subproblems are solved locally at each iteration, dual variables are updated, and convergence is checked via the worst primal residual.


\subsection{General ADMM-Based OPF Solvers}
\label{subsec:admm_solvers}

We have developed two open-source general-purpose solvers for distributed OPF problems, one for DC OPF and another for AC OPF. Both solvers adopt ADMM to coordinate regional solutions and ensure consistency across tie-lines. 

\subsubsection{DPLib DC OPF Solver}
\label{subsec:dcopf}

The DPLib DC OPF solves the OPF problem using ADMM by optimizing each region independently and enforcing agreement on shared variables (tie-line voltage angles) through iterative dual updates. The solver is available at \href{https://github.com/LSU-RAISE-LAB/DPLib/tree/main/DPLib%20toolkit/DPLib_DCOPF_Solver}{GitHub}, including the MATLAB implementation file named \texttt{DistDCOPF.m}. Designed for partitioned MATPOWER cases, it requires MATLAB R2020a or newer with MATPOWER and YALMIP installed. Upon execution, the user provides the partitioned \texttt{.mat} file, ADMM penalty parameter \( \rho \), convergence threshold, maximum number of iterations, and optionally the centralized OPF cost for benchmarking via optimality gap. 

After receiving user inputs, the code loads all regional and tie-line data, augments each region’s branch matrix with inter-regional tie-lines, and initializes bus voltage angle variables and indexing. The core ADMM loop then solves each region’s DC OPF using YALMIP, minimizing generation cost under local constraints (e.g., power balance, generator limits) while adding consistency penalties on shared tie-line angles as modeled in \eqref{dcopf}. A helper function handles the regional problem formulation and solution.

After each iteration, the code evaluates tie-line angle consistency and updates dual variables accordingly. Primal residuals are recorded, and convergence is declared if the maximum error drops below the user-defined threshold; otherwise, the process continues until the iteration limit. The optimality gap is computed and plotted alongside residuals if a centralized cost is provided. Progress and convergence status are printed in the command window.


The DPLib DC OPF solver supports any number of regions and is tailored for use with partitioned \texttt{.mat} files, enabling reproducible distributed OPF testing. Algorithm~\ref{alg:opf} summarizes its workflow. 


\begin{algorithm}[t!]
\caption{DPLib OPF solver}\label{alg:opf}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
    \STATE \textbf{Input:} Partitioned MATPOWER data, ADMM parameters \( \rho \), convergence tolerance \( \epsilon \), maximum iterations
    \STATE Load the partitioned case data for all regions
    \STATE Augment each region with its corresponding tie-line data
    \STATE Initialize region variables including local decision variables and dual variables
    \STATE Retrieve ADMM parameters and consistency constraints
    \STATE Identify and prepare interface buses at region boundaries
    \FOR{each ADMM iteration}
        \STATE Solve local OPF for each region independently
        \STATE Update dual variables based on consistency violations at bordering buses
        \IF {convergence criteria are met}
            \STATE \textbf{break}
        \ENDIF
    \ENDFOR
    \STATE Visualize and save final solution
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}


\subsubsection{DPLib AC OPF Solver}
\label{sec:acopf_code} 

The DPLib AC OPF solver implements a distributed ADMM-based solver in MATLAB for any power system preprocessed using the DPLib partitioning tool. It enables large-scale distributed analysis, where each region independently solves its local AC OPF while coordinating inter-regional tie-lines. The solver developed in this work is publicly available at \href{https://github.com/LSU-RAISE-LAB/DPLib/tree/main/DPLib%20toolkit/DPLib_ACOPF_Solver}{GitHub}, including the MATLAB implementation file named \texttt{DistACOPF.m}. To run the code, the user must have MATLAB R2020a or later, with MATPOWER and IPOPT properly configured. Upon launch, the code prompts for the partitioned \texttt{.mat} file, ADMM penalty parameter \( \rho \), convergence threshold, maximum number of iterations, and optionally the centralized OPF cost for benchmarking via optimality gap. 

After loading the partitioned data, the code augments each region’s branch matrix with its tie-lines and initializes region-specific variables (e.g., bus counts, initial voltage values). The main ADMM loop iteratively solves each region’s local AC OPF via the helper function \texttt{Acopf}, based on \eqref{acopf}, updating voltage magnitudes and angles. Dual variables are adjusted using consistency gradients to enforce agreement across tie-lines. Convergence is declared if the worst primal residual falls below the threshold. The optimality gap is computed per iteration if a centralized cost is provided. Final plots show convergence trends, and status messages are printed throughout. The overall process flow of the DPLib AC OPF solver is presented in algorithm \ref{alg:opf}. 

\section{DPLib: Benchmark Test Cases}\label{sec:case_studies}

Distributed versions of many widely used MATPOWER benchmark systems are generated. Each test case is processed using the DPLib graph-based partitioning tool and tested using the DPLib ADMM-based DC and AC OPF solvers described earlier. Across all systems, both solvers successfully converge to feasible solutions. All partitioned cases and solver outputs are publicly available as part of the DPLib at \href{https://github.com/LSU-RAISE-LAB/DPLib.git}{GitHub}. In the following subsections, we explain some of the distributed test cases generated and tested as benchmarks in DPLib.

In addition to evaluating convergence using the worst-case primal residual and the optimality gap, further validation tests are performed on the generated multi-region systems to verify their consistency with physical laws and operational constraints of the power grid. Specifically, we verified that upon the convergence of the ADMM iterations, the total power generation across all regions closely matches the total system demand, accounting for minor discrepancies due to modeling approximations and convergence thresholds. Furthermore, we confirmed that active power flows across each inter-regional tie-line are consistent from both sides of the interface. All tested datasets successfully passed these validation checks, further demonstrating the correctness and reliability of the generated partitioned cases. 

To facilitate evaluation of the benchmark test cases using OPF solvers, all primal variables were initialized with cold-start conditions: voltage angles were set to zero and voltage magnitudes to one per unit. The dual variables, corresponding to Lagrange multipliers associated with consistency constraints, were initialized to one. Penalty parameters \(\rho\) in the ADMM-based solvers were selected case by case to ensure convergence.


