we first need to select the most important terms from \eqref{eq:f_Riesz_d}, which get reconstructed by the neural network.
This is captured by the concept of best $n$-term approximation, a concept,
which seems to go back as far as to \cite{Schmidt} and which is frequently used in nonlinear approximation theory \cite{DeVore}.

Let $X\subset Y$ 
be two Banach spaces and let $\Phi\subset X$ be a set of elements of $X$.
For $f\in X$ and a positive integer $n\ge 1$, we denote
\[
% \sigma_n(x):=\sigma_n(x,X,Y,\Phi)=\inf\left\{\left\|x-\sum_{j=1}^n \alpha_j\varphi_j\right\|_Y:\alpha_1,\dots,\alpha_n\in\C, \varphi_1,\dots,\varphi_n\in \Phi\right\}
\sigma_n(f):=\sigma_n(f,Y,\Phi)=\inf\left\{\left\|f-\sum_{j=1}^n \alpha_j\varphi_j\right\|_Y:\alpha_1,\dots,\alpha_n\in\C, \varphi_1,\dots,\varphi_n\in \Phi\right\}
\]

and
\[
% \sigma_n:=
\sigma_n(X,Y,\Phi):=\sup_{\|f\|_X\le 1}\sigma_n(f).
\]
Here, we only consider the case where $X$ and $Y$ are Banach spaces of sequences, 
in which case we always choose $\Phi:=\{e_n\}$ the set of canonical sequences with $(e_n)_j=1$ if $n=j$ and $(e_n)_j=0$ otherwise,
and we write $\sigma_n(X,Y)$ for $\sigma_n(X,Y,\Phi)$.

