\subsection{Experimental Details}
\label{sec:exp-details}

With \ours{}, we evaluate 14 representative vision-language models (VLMs) spanning a range of sizes, model families, and access levels. These include \textbf{(1) multimodal Large Reasoning Models (MLRMs)}, such as Claude-3.7-Sonnet~\cite{claude3_7}; \textbf{(2) proprietary multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs)}, such as GPT-4o~\cite{hurst2024gpt}; and (3) \textbf{open-source MLLMs}, such as the Qwen-2.5-VL series~\cite{bai2025qwen2}. The complete list of models used in our experiments is provided in Table~\ref{tab:model_cards}.

We evaluate two representative paradigms in misinformation detection, distinguished by their reasoning focus:
\textbf{(1)} The \textbf{implication-oriented (I)} approach, which focuses on the inferred implications of the news sample; and
\textbf{(2)} The \textbf{consistency-oriented (C)} approach, which assesses consistency both across modalities and between the image–caption pair and the reference article. \textbf{\textit{Unless otherwise specified, all experiments on \ours{} default to the consistency-oriented (C) approach.}} Experimental setup and evaluation prompt details are shown in Appendix~\ref{app:exp-setup}.


\subsection{Main Results}

\noindent \textbf{Consistency-Oriented Reasoning Generally Outperforms Implication-Oriented Reasoning.} As shown in Table \ref{tab:main_results}, a possible reason is that misleading news is often constructed through deliberate content manipulation. Detecting unsubstantiated inconsistencies, either between the image and its caption or between the image-caption pair and the reference article, helps models better identify misleading intent.

