\section{Introduction}

Despite more than a decade of research on machine learning for malware detection, the problem remains far from solved. For example, \tesseract~\cite{pendlebury2019tesseract}, inspired by other works~\cite{allix2015your,miller2016reviewer}, demonstrated the impact of concept drift on malware classifiers, and provided constraints and suggestions to eliminate experimental bias and have more realistic evaluations.

The \androzoo~\cite{alecci2024androzoo,allix2016androzoo,androzoourl} project generated new hope among researchers to have more realistic, large-scale evaluations. Periodically, \androzoo crawls applications from multiple markets (\eg \googleplay, AppChina), and as of today provides the largest data source of \acp{apk}, with over 25 millions apps from over 15 years. When crawled, \acp{apk} are submitted to the \longvt~\cite{vturl} service, which runs apps against multiple \ac{AV} engines and produce detailed analysis reports (\eg with family information). 

These repositories and frameworks, such as \tesseract, advocating guidelines for evaluations in non-stationary security contexts~\cite{pendlebury2019tesseract,miller2016reviewer,allix2015your,dos_and_donts,flood2024bad}, have enabled more realistic, time-aware evaluations of \ac{ML} methods for Android malware detection. \textit{Or have they?}



To challenge this assumption, let us consider two state-of-the-art datasets used in top-tier security venues and sampled from \androzoo: \textit{APIGraph}~\cite{apigraph_paper} and \textit{Transcendent}~\cite{barbero2022transcending}.
Both datasets strictly abide by all constraints outlined in \tesseract~\cite{pendlebury2019tesseract} to eliminate spatial and temporal bias, and align with all best practices for dataset construction and evaluation~\cite{dos_and_donts,flood2024bad}. Surprisingly, evaluation across five state-of-the-art malware detectors reveals substantial discrepancy in \fone on the two datasets (see \autoref{fig:motivational_plot}), with detection performance on \textit{APIGraph} being consistently higher than on \textit{Transcendent} across the same time frame. We postulate that the spatio-temporal constraints in prior work~\cite{pendlebury2019tesseract,dos_and_donts,flood2024bad,sommer2010outside} do not entirely eliminate experimental bias, and that there are other nuanced factors to consider. 

More specifically, we identify five new spatio-temporal factors that can affect dataset sampling and evaluation of a learning-based Android malware detector. 
After introducing our hypotheses, we empirically evaluate the impact of each factor using five representative state-of-the-art Android malware classifiers: \drebin~\cite{arp2014drebin}, \deepdrebin~\cite{deepdrebin}, \malscan~\cite{malscan_paper}, \ramda~\cite{ramda}, and \HCC~\cite{chen2023continuous}. Based on our findings, we provide practical recommendations for each of the five factors to reduce spatial and temporal biases, demonstrating how to practically apply our recommendations in a newly-sampled dataset in~\autoref{sec:casestudy}. 

In summary, we make the following contributions:
\begin{itemize}[noitemsep]
    \item We discover five novel spatio-temporal bias factors (\autoref{sec:factor-analysis}) beyond \tesseract~\cite{pendlebury2019tesseract}, which can lead to misleading results even if following all current recommendations for realistic evaluations~\cite{flood2024bad,thirumuruganathan2024detecting,pendlebury2019tesseract,dos_and_donts,sommer2010outside}, as shown in \autoref{fig:motivational_plot}. 
    \item We systematically analyze the impact of these factors on five state-of-the-art Android classifiers~\cite{arp2014drebin,deepdrebin,malscan_paper,ramda,chen2023continuous}), and two datasets~\cite{apigraph_paper,barbero2022transcending}.Where necessary, we augment our analysis by sampling additional data from \androzoo to further generalize our findings outside of \textit{Transcendent} and \textit{APIGraph} datasets.

    \item We showcase the practicality of our recommendations by performing a case study on a newly sampled dataset between 2021--2023 (\autoref{sec:casestudy}), that follows our sampling recommendations and evaluation guidelines.

\end{itemize}

