\section{Preliminary Observations}\label{sec:preliminary-observations}


We aim to investigate the reasons behind the striking performance difference observed in~\autoref{fig:motivational_plot}, regardless of the feature representation or classifier used. 

We focus our attention on two representative Android datasets published in top-tier security conferences:
\textit{APIGraph}~\cite{apigraph_paper} (\apigraph) and \textit{Transcendent}~\cite{barbero2022transcending} (\transcend). 
We selected these datasets as they adhere to all spatial and temporal constraints outlined in \tesseract~\cite{pendlebury2019tesseract} and align with recommendations from Arp et al.~\cite{dos_and_donts}. Moreover, they overlap in 2014--2018; hence, we restrict \apigraph to this period to match the time frame of \transcend, allowing us to perform direct comparison between the two datasets. 

\paragraph{Family Overlap} Prior work~\cite{chow2023drift} showed that the appearance of new malware families were the major cause of drift in \transcend. Hence,  we investigate the distribution differences between the two datasets by computing the ``family overlap,'' \ie the percentage of malware families for each testing year 2015--2018 that existed during the training year 2014; a more formal definition can be found in~\autoref{sec:appendix:family-overlap-formula}. \autoref{fig:apigraph_transcendent_family_overlap} shows that new malware families steadily replace older ones in \transcend, while malware family overlap does not show a clear trend in \apigraph. Considering that both datasets were sampled within the same time frame, we postulate that certain factors differed during the curation of the dataset, resulting in the different distributions of malware samples.


