\paragraph{Novel Spatio-Temporal Bias Factors} To identify possible sources of bias that explain the difference in performance in \autoref{fig:motivational_plot}, we first examine the high-level statistics of \apigraph and \transcend. We summarize the main differences identified in~\autoref{tab:datasets_differences}. Based on these differences, we hypothesize five novel spatio-temporal bias factors that have been overlooked in previous work and may cause the observed performance gap. The \textit{App Markets} affect the sampling of both goodware and malware apps, whereas higher labeling \textit{VT Thresholds} may lead to malware that is ``easier to detect.'' While \textit{Dataset Size} is similar between \apigraph and \transcend, it needs to be investigated whether it is statistically significant and can be expected to reliably reflect the underlying distribution. \textit{Timestamp Types} may affect both the sampling and the evaluation phases, as they may result in a dissimilar distribution. Finally, while the \textit{Time Frame} of the two datasets is the same, we argue that existing metrics such as Area Under Time (AUT)~\cite{pendlebury2019tesseract} capture only a temporal snapshot of performance, and may be the result of a lucky time frame---we call this bias factor \textit{Temporal Luck}. 

In the remainder of this paper, we investigate how the identified factors interplay and may affect realistic evaluations. Furthermore, we provide recommendations on how to prevent them. 


