\section{Experimental Settings} \label{sec:experimental-setup}
We first provide a brief overview of the datasets, classifiers, and metrics used in the remainder of the paper. 

\paragraph{Datasets}
To investigate the four identified factors, we use both \apigraph~\cite{apigraph_paper} and \transcend~\cite{barbero2022transcending} (see~\autoref{sec:preliminary-observations}). Where appropriate, we generalize our findings on the wider Android malware population by utilizing metadata from \androzoo~\cite{androzoourl}, obtaining reports from \longvt~\cite{vturl} and unifying family labels from individual \ac{AV} engines using Euphony~\cite{hurier2017euphony}. Finally, we demonstrate how to practically apply our sampling and evaluation guidelines from~\autoref{sec:factor-analysis} by collecting and evaluating a recent dataset from 2021--2023 in~\autoref{sec:casestudy}. More details about the datasets used can be found in the appendix~\autoref{sec:appendix:datasets}.


\paragraph{Classifiers}
All our experiments are carried out on five representative Android malware classifiers:

\begin{itemize}[noitemsep]
\item \textbf{\drebin}\cite{arp2014drebin}: Linear SVM using binary features from static analysis (e.g., APIs, URLs, Activities).
\item \textbf{\deepdrebin}\cite{deepdrebin}: Uses the same features as \drebin, with an MLP classifier.
\item \textbf{\malscan}\cite{malscan_paper}: Graph-based approach using degree centrality with a Random Forest classifier.
\item \textbf{\ramda}\cite{ramda}: DNN model combining VAE and MLP, focusing on sensitive APIs, Permissions, and Intents.
\item \textbf{\HCC}~\cite{chen2023continuous}: Autoencoder-guided embedding using malware family labels for enhanced class separability.
\end{itemize}
These classifier represent diverse feature representations and model architectures: token-based (\drebin, \deepdrebin, \HCC), API-based (\ramda), and graph-based (\malscan); and models including linear SVM, MLP, autoencoder, DNN, and Random Forest. This heterogeneity allows for robust, representation-agnostic insights. Notably, \HCC~\cite{chen2023continuous} builds upon \drebin and \deepdrebin and represents the current state-of-the-art.

\paragraph{Metrics}
For each factor, we assess their impact using a combination of standard performance metric, the \fone, the \textit{AUT} metric for temporal evaluation~\cite{pendlebury2019tesseract}, and the \textit{Family Overlap} measure introduced in~\autoref{sec:preliminary-observations} to observe the distribution shift of malware families independent from the feature representation or classifier used (see Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix:family-overlap-formula}).

\section{Novel Spatio-Temporal Bias Factors} \label{sec:factor-analysis}
Following the observations in~\autoref{sec:preliminary-observations}, we introduce five novel factors for spatio-temporal experimental biases: \textbf{\factorTimestampName}~(\autoref{sec:factor-timestamps}), \textbf{\factorTemporalLuckName}~(\autoref{sec:factor-temporal-luck}), \textbf{\factorMarketsName}~(\autoref{sec:factor-app-markets}), \textbf{\factorVTTName}~(\autoref{sec:factor-vtt}), and \textbf{\factorSamplingName}~(\autoref{sec:factor-sampling-size}). For each factor, we begin with a hypothesis, then evaluate its impact, and finally provide practical recommendations.


\subsection{Timestamp Types} \label{sec:factor-timestamps}
\subsubsection{Hypothesis} 
\acp{apk} often have multiple associated timestamps, such as file creation and market publication dates. These correspond to different points in time in the \ac{apk}’s life cycle. For longitudinal evaluation, the choice of timestamps for temporal slicing affects when an \ac{apk} is thought to have appeared in the real world. We focus on two main types: creation timestamps, which are locally embedded and potentially unreliable, and publication timestamps, which reflect when an app was made available to users.



\hypothesisbox{\textbf{Hypothesis: \factorTimestampName}}{The choice of timestamp type (creation vs. publication) affects the temporal distribution of malicious \acp{apk} in the sampled dataset, which in turn impacts performance evaluation. In particular, using unreliable timestamps (e.g., creation date) may misrepresent when \acp{apk} or threats actually appeared.}

