\subsubsection{Impact}\label{sec:timestamps-impact} 
We find that creation timestamps are an unreliable proxy for the actual release time, often deviating significantly from publication timestamps. As a result, using creation dates can shift \acp{apk} outside from their true temporal context, leading to incorrect assumptions on the time a threat emerged. This temporal misalignment can distort sampling and, consequently, affect performance evaluations.

\paragraph{Approximating Creation and Publication Timestamps} First, we need to decide how to approximate creation and publication timestamps. \transcend~\cite{barbero2022transcending} uses the \dexdate, \ie the filesystem timestamp that indicates when the \textit{classes.dex} file in an \ac{apk} was last modified. \dexdates have been commonly used to approximate creation dates for time-aware evaluations~\cite{pendlebury2019tesseract,allix2015your}. Instead, \apigraph~\cite{apigraph_paper} uses the \vtdate, the timestamp that indicates when an \ac{apk} was first submitted to \acl{vt}. Conceptually, \vtdates should be considered publication timestamps, as they indicate when a sample was first submitted to \ac{vt}. However, we observed that there are minimal differences between \dexdates and \vtdates. As shown in~\autoref{fig:dex_dates_comparison}, reordering \apigraph and \transcend using the other dataset timestamp type: (that is, \transcend sorted by \vtdates and \apigraph by \dexdates) results in the vast majority of the samples lying on the diagonal (or in its proximity). This suggests that samples are generally submitted to \shortvt shortly after their build date. Therefore, we classify \vtdates as creation timestamps. As \googleplay dates became available on \androzoo after June 2020~\cite{alecci2024androzoo} and the time frame of \transcend and \apigraph precedes that, we require an approximation of publication timestamps for evaluations prior to 2020. We note that the \androzoo \crawldates for \googleplay represent an exceptionally accurate approximation of official \googleplay publication dates, with a median difference of only 5 days~\footnote{This statistic was computed on a population of $10,398,144$ samples in \androzoo.} between the publication of an \ac{apk} on \googleplay and its inclusion in \androzoo. Hence, we refer to \crawldates as an approximation for publication timestamps.



\paragraph{Timestamps Impact on Sampling}
We now evaluate whether creation timestamps or publication timestamps produce more realistic data distributions. To this end, we perform experiments exclusively on \transcend, since most of the information needed to perform the same evaluations on \apigraph is not available. More specifically, \androzoo's \crawldates for \apigraph would indicate that most samples are from 2018, and VirusShare, from which \apigraph malware is sampled, is no longer accessible due to severe hardware faults (as of May/June 2025).

Since goodware has minimal effect on the drift of \transcend~\cite{chow2023drift}, we focus on the impact that timestamps have on the distribution of malware samples. We imitated the sampling described in~\cite{barbero2022transcending}, which used \dexdates (creation timestamps). We identically and independently (randomly) sample five malware datasets from the full \androzoo population using the sampling criteria of \transcend: time frame 2014--2018, \ac{vtt} $\geq 4$, \dexdates as timestamps, and an identical number of malicious samples per month, accumulating to $26,387$ malicious \acp{apk} per dataset. We then analyzed their ``family overlaps'' in \autoref{fig:transcend_2025} (as in \autoref{sec:preliminary-observations}), from which we notice a stark difference between $\mathcal{D}_{vtt4}^{2025}$ and \transcend. We hypothesize that this is due to the fact that \androzoo has changed significantly since \transcend was originally sampled. Specifically, we observe that several malicious \acp{apk} with creation timestamps older than their publication dates were added to \androzoo since the release of \transcend. This suggests that malware samples with \dexdates belonging to that period were added long after the dataset was sampled.
%
To further confirm our reasoning, we contacted the authors of~\cite{barbero2022transcending} to find out the date when \transcend was sampled. They informed us that the first three years 2014--2016 correspond to the dataset published with~\cite{pendlebury2019tesseract} and were sampled in early 2018, while the last two years 2017--2018 were sampled in early 2019 as part of the dataset released with~\cite{pierazzi2020intriguing}. 
%
By analyzing the \androzoo metadata for the samples in \transcend, we notice that the youngest sample in 2014--2016 was added to \androzoo in April 2017 and that the youngest in 2017--2018 in January 2019. Given this information, we repeat the previous experiment filtering out all samples with \dexdate between 2014--2016 whose \crawldate is subsequent to mid-2017 and all samples with \dexdate between 2017--2018 whose \crawldate indicates that the samples were added to \androzoo after January 2019. In this way, we try to emulate the sampling process of \cite{pendlebury2019tesseract} and \cite{pierazzi2020intriguing}. 

\autoref{fig:transcend_2025} compares the average yearly family overlap for the datasets we sampled. It can be seen that the average family overlap for the datasets sampled in 2025 differs from that of the datasets sampled trying to emulate~\cite{pendlebury2019tesseract,pierazzi2020intriguing}, the latter approaching that of \transcend. 


