\paragraph{Timestamps Impact on Performance}
We now measure the impact of creation and publication timestamps on the performance of the five classifiers we introduced in~\autoref{sec:experimental-setup}. As aforementioned, we limit the evaluation to \transcend due to missing information for \apigraph.


Hence, we evaluate all five classifiers on \transcend, training on 2016 and testing in 2017--2018, using the \crawldate to reorder \acp{apk} in the dataset. We eliminate 2014--2015 because \androzoo had just been created in that period; hence, not many samples were added to the repository in that time frame. Specifically, no samples in \transcend were added to \androzoo in 2015. \autoref{fig:timestamp_classifier_performance} shows the performance difference when using \dexdates versus \crawldates. It is evident that all classifiers exhibit more drift throughout 2017 and the first half of 2018 when using \crawldates. In contrast, the performance remains stable for all classifiers when using \dexdates.

\subsubsection{Recommendations}
Given the unreliability of creation timestamps, we advocate against using them, in contrast with previous published work~\cite{pendlebury2019tesseract,pierazzi2020intriguing,barbero2022transcending,apigraph_paper}. Instead, we recommend using publication timestamps as these describe exactly when samples started circulating and are therefore more reliable. In particular, one should use the dates indicating when an \ac{apk} was uploaded to a market. For Google Play, this corresponds to the ``last update date'' available for each app. This information is also provided and recommended by \androzoo for \acp{apk} downloaded from \googleplay after June 2020~\cite{alecci2024androzoo}.

\recommendationboxnew{Recommendation: \factorTimestampName}{Datasets \textbf{must} be sampled using publication timestamps to model the temporal distribution. Official market upload dates should be preferred (\eg \googleplay publication dates); otherwise, \crawldates from \androzoo can be an approximation.}


\subsection{Temporal Luck}\label{sec:factor-temporal-luck}
\subsubsection{Hypothesis}
Two datasets $D_1$ and $D_2$, sampled from different time frames (\eg 2012--2014 and 2016--2018), can be expected to represent different distributions (\eg they may vary in terms of family composition). Therefore, no meaningful conclusions can be drawn from comparing the performance of a malware classifier on $D_1$ with the performance of another malware detection method evaluated on $D_2$. This issue of comparing malware detection methods on different datasets presenting a distribution mismatch often arises when researchers directly compare their results with those reported in other related papers (``comparisons on paper'').

Given two malware detection approaches $A_1$ and $A_2$, a straightforward solution would be to evaluate both on the \textit{same} dataset, as done in recent work (\eg ~\cite{apigraph_paper,malscan_paper,barbero2022transcending}). Nevertheless, this may not be sufficient to conclude which method performs best, as the reported results may still be influenced by what we call \textit{\factorTemporalLuckName}. This phenomenon arises when an arbitrary time-aware split into training and testing inflates the performance of a given method, due to the training set, by chance, being ``exceptionally'' good or the subsequent testing period not drifting significantly. In practical terms, given a dataset $\mathcal{D}_{2012-2020}$, $A_1$ may perform better than $A_2$ when training on 2012 and testing on 2013--2020, whereas $A_2$ may exhibit superior performance when training on 2013 and testing on 2014--2020. Hence, we postulate the following hypothesis.

\hypothesisbox{\textbf{Hypothesis: \factorTemporalLuckName}}{Given a dataset, the performance of a malware detection method may vary based on the temporal train/test splits.}


