\subsubsection{Impact}
\autoref{fig:temporal_luck_motivation} provides an overview of the impact of \factorTemporalLuckName on both \apigraph and \transcend. The settings we employ are the following: we train classifiers on data from each year in the time frame 2014--2018 and test them in the years that follow. In practice, we divide the datasets into four subsets: the first having 2014 as training window and testing on 2015--2018, the second having 2015 for training and 2016--2018 for testing, and so on. It is visually evident in \transcend that significantly different performance profiles may be obtained depending on the temporal split. This can also be observed for \apigraph, albeit to a lesser extent.

To quantify \factorTemporalLuckName in \apigraph and \transcend, we compute the \acf{AUT} score when training on one year and testing only on the following year (\ie (i) train on 2014 and test on 2015, (ii) train on 2015 and test on 2016, etc.). The \ac{AUT} summarizes the performance of a classifier over a certain time frame, and in the presence of \factorTemporalLuckName, it can emphasize when a proposed approach is consistently working across different temporal splits. The results are summarized in \autoref{tab:aut_by_year}. We can again observe significantly different results on \transcend for all classifiers and temporal configurations, and smaller differences for \apigraph (with a larger difference in the last column).

While revealing substantial performance variation across training years, the \ac{AUT} may complicate the comparison between different state-of-the-art classifiers, as no individual classifier clearly performs better than the others in all scenarios. For instance, \drebin performs better than \deepdrebin in 2014--2015 and 2016--2017 on \transcend. The reverse is true for the other two temporal configurations. \HCC, the current state-of-the-art in Android malware detection, shows the highest \ac{AUT} in 2016--2017 and 2017--2018 on \apigraph only. Given these mixed results, it is legitimate to wonder which one should be considered the superior classifier.

To facilitate direct comparison between different malware detection methods, we propose the following extension of the \ac{AUT} metric, providing a compact summary of the time-aware performance of a given classifier (given a metric $f$, \eg \fone), while accounting for \factorTemporalLuckName:


\begin{align}
    \text{A-AUT}(f,\mathcal{C},N|\mathcal{D}) = (\mu_{AUT}(f,\mathcal{C},N|\mathcal{D}), \nonumber \\ \sigma_{AUT}(f,\mathcal{C},N|\mathcal{D})) 
\label{eq:avg_aut}
\end{align}

 \begin{equation}
    \mu_{AUT}(f,\mathcal{C},N|\mathcal{D})  = \frac{1}{|\mathcal{C}|} \sum_{c=1}^{|\mathcal{C}|} AUT(f,N|\mathcal{D}_c)  
    \label{eq:mu-aut}
 \end{equation}


\autoref{eq:avg_aut} presents the \acf{A-AUT}, which is defined as the arithmetic mean ($\mu_{AUT}$ in \autoref{eq:mu-aut}) and standard deviation ($\sigma_{AUT}$) of the \ac{AUT} scores computed by dividing a dataset $\mathcal{D}$ into temporally successive datasets $\{\mathcal{D}_1, \cdots, \mathcal{D}_{|\mathcal{C}|}\}$, which are obtained by shifting forward the training set by $N$ months. Each test set is comprised of all the samples belonging to the following $N$ months. The parameter $\mathcal{C}$ in Equation~\ref{eq:avg_aut} is the set of all classifiers produced by training the model under analysis on the training set of each $d\in \{\mathcal{D}_1, \cdots, \mathcal{D}_{|\mathcal{C}|}\}$. A practical example of this \textit{rolling window} approach is provided in \autoref{sec:appendix:tempora_luck_example}. 


