In Table~\ref{tab:avg_aut}, we report the A-AUT (\ie $\mu_{AUT}$ and $\sigma_{AUT}$) of the $F_1$-Score computed for our considered classifiers and datasets. The proposed metric facilitates the direct comparison of different malware detection methods, while accounting for temporal luck. For instance, it can help us determine which classifier between \drebin and \deepdrebin performs the best on \transcend: \drebin shows a slightly better performance than \deepdrebin ($\mu_{AUT}=0.70$ for the first, $\mu_{AUT}=0.68$ for the second) while also guaranteeing a ``more stable'' performance across different temporal splits ($\sigma_{AUT}=0.06$ for \drebin against $\sigma_{AUT}=0.11$ for \deepdrebin). And \malscan is worse than both of them, with higher variability. For both datasets, \HCC results as the best performing classifier overall.


\subsubsection{Recommendation}
As a single train-test split may unfairly (dis)advantage one classifier over another. We recommend using the proposed \ac{A-AUT} metric to provide a less biased mean estimate of the classifier performance, along with a measure of performance stability over time splits. 

\recommendationboxnew{Recommendation: \factorTemporalLuckName}{
 You \textbf{must} use \ac{A-AUT} as a metric to compare malware detection methods.
 If multiple methods have comparable \acp{A-AUT}, you \textbf{must} provide an AUT breakdown. You \textbf{may} use \ac{A-AUT} with $N=12$ months, to have a yearly summary of the results.}


\subsection{App Markets}\label{sec:factor-app-markets}
\subsubsection{Hypothesis}

Android apps are distributed across multiple markets, each serving different regions and user bases. For example, \googleplay is the dominant platform in Western regions, while alternative markets are more prevalent in Eastern contexts. When constructing malware datasets, researchers often sample from a single market—typically \googleplay{}—especially when focusing on region-specific threats or working within the constraints of public data (see~\autoref{sec:casestudy}). While this yields datasets representative of their target market, it may limit generalizability. In contrast, recent studies~\cite{pendlebury2019tesseract,barbero2022transcending,apigraph_paper} adopt multi-market sampling to increase diversity and scale. However, such strategies may unintentionally introduce shortcut learning, as classifiers could learn market-correlated features instead of security-relevant ones~\cite{dos_and_donts}.

\autoref{tab:markets_metadata_azoo_datasets} shows the market composition of the full \androzoo, \apigraph and \transcend datasets, based on metadata from \androzoo~\cite{androzoourl}. The data reveal substantial imbalance: certain markets contribute disproportionately to the \androzoo population, and the \apigraph and \transcend datasets differ distinctly in their sampling. In particular, \apigraph exhibits a pronounced sampling bias, sourcing malware primarily from VirusShare and goodware from \googleplay.

We therefore hypothesize that an imbalanced market composition in training data, where malware and goodware are predominantly sampled from different markets, results in statistically significant-degradation of classifier performance when evaluated on market-balanced or differently-composed test sets. This is consistent with previous findings on shortcut learning~\cite{dos_and_donts} and is testable using market metadata in \androzoo.

\hypothesisbox{Hypothesis: \textbf{\factorMarketsName}}{Altering the composition of \factorMarketsName used in dataset sampling significantly affects goodware and malware class-distribution, and leads to reduced detection performance (\eg \fone or AUT) when models are evaluated on out-of-sample market distributions.}

