\paragraph{Similarity of \acp{apk}}
We begin by analyzing the impact on performance when training and testing are conducted on the same markets and on different markets. As shown in~\autoref{tab:data_market_results}, training and testing on the same market consistently yields higher \fone scores compared to cross-market scenarios. For example, \drebin achieves an increase of 0.551 and 0.361 in \fone score when evaluated on the same market. These results indicate notable differences in the characteristics of goodware and malware across market groups.

This point is further underscored when examining the \fone of classifiers trained on samples from one source, but tested on $\mathcal{D}_{EVEN}$ (samples from GP and 3PM). For example, training on $\mathcal{D}_{GP}$ and testing on $\mathcal{D}_{EVEN}$ yields \fone of 0.395 and 0.511 for \deepdrebin and \malscan, respectively—significantly lower than the scores observed when testing on $\mathcal{D}_{GP}$ alone. Similar trends are observed for $\mathcal{D}_{3PM}$ and are consistent for all classifiers.

\paragraph{Combining markets for training/testing}
Prior work~\cite{pendlebury2019tesseract,barbero2022transcending,apigraph_paper} has adopted multi-market sampling. Here we analyze training on mixed markets and testing on mixed or single markets. We find that, regardless of the proportion of Google Play to 3PM apps (e.g., 1:1 or 4:1), the \fone lies within the range of testing on single markets.

\paragraph{Goodware and Malware from different markets}
Dataset compositions that include goodware and malware from distinct markets lead to unrealistically-high \fone. This aligns with findings from~\cite{dos_and_donts}, where classifiers inadvertently learn spurious correlations, distinguishing app origins rather than actual differences between goodware and malware. These results clearly demonstrate the influence of app markets on classifier performance and the risk of introducing unintentional biases.

\paragraph{Takeaways}
Arp et al.~\cite{dos_and_donts} showed that sampling malware and goodware from different markets can lead to spurious correlations. We extend this finding by showing apps from different markets are inherently different (even if class ratio stays consistent) and training on one market does not transfer well to another. Finally, we observed that testing on mixed markets may give unclear performance that skews towards the majority market.

\subsubsection{Recommendations} It is imperative that the sampling is not biased such that goodware and malware originate from different markets. To ensure this, one should sample goodware and malware from the same market. If you wish to use multiple markets, you must sample from each separately, and test only on single markets to obtain the best and worst case. 

\recommendationboxnew{Recommendation: \factorMarketsName}{The \factorMarketsName distribution \textbf{must} be consistent between goodware and malware apps, to avoid spatial bias. When performing multi-market evaluation, you \textbf{must} sample from each market separately, and report the single-market test performance to obtain the best and worst case.}






\subsection{VirusTotal Threshold} \label{sec:factor-vtt}
\subsubsection{Hypothesis}
Although \ac{vtt} is generally considered by the community as a labeling tool, in pratice it also acts as a sampling parameter. For example, it excludes all samples that have a number of detections between 1 and the chosen \ac{vtt} value from the base population (these samples are usually called \textit{greyware}). For this reason, we expect \ac{vtt} to play a role in the distribution of malware samples within a dataset. This is particularly evident in \androzoo, for which we posit that selecting different \ac{vtt} values significantly affects the population from which to sample, leading to different malware distributions.

\hypothesisbox{Hypothesis: \factorVTTName}{The choice of \ac{vtt} affects the distribution from which Android malware is sampled, because it acts as a sampling parameter which filters the original population.}


\subsubsection{Impact}


Starting from our preliminary observation in~\autoref{sec:preliminary-observations}, we investigate the impact of \ac{vtt} on malware distribution. To this end, we use the two sampling configurations described in~\autoref{sec:timestamps-impact}: (i) we sample in 2025 using \transcend sampling criteria; (ii) we emulate the sampling of~\cite{pendlebury2019tesseract} and~\cite{pierazzi2020intriguing} by sampling in mid-2017 and 2019 for 2014--2016 and 2017--2018, respectively. We repeat both configurations with \ac{vtt}=4 and \ac{vtt}=15.

\autoref{fig:vtt_4_15} shows the family overlaps we obtained for all 4 configurations. Interestingly, we notice there is minimal difference between \ac{vtt}=4 and \ac{vtt}=15, when you compare orange and blue bars. This suggest that \ac{vtt} does not \textit{directly} influence the distribution of a sampled dataset. 
However, when observing the distribution of malware in different app markets, we notice that \ac{vtt} does have an impact. \autoref{fig:vtt_to_market_heatmap} shows the percentage of samples from each market when using a different \acp{vtt}. By picking a higher \ac{vtt}, one would inadvertently sample more malware from one specific market. In~\autoref{sec:factor-app-markets}, we showed that this could lead to spurious correlations and one should ensure goodware and malware are sampled from the same market. Furthermore, we observe that using a higher vtt will not only only affect distribution of markets but also reduce the population of available malware (\eg \ac{vtt}=4 captures 44\% of all malware in \androzoo, whereas \ac{vtt}=15 captures 14.11\%).

