\subsubsection{Recommendations} We note that \ac{vtt} does not directly affect the family overlap of malicious samples. However, it does affect the markets the malware is sampled from and, consequently, the performance (see~\autoref{sec:factor-app-markets}) and the amount of malware available in the wild. Therefore, the lower the \ac{vtt}, the more representative the sampled dataset would be. Given the finding of~\cite{miller2016reviewer}, namely that a minimum of 4 detections is required to reduce noisy labels, we recommend to use \ac{vtt}=4. 

\recommendationboxnew{\textbf{Recommendation: \factorVTTName}}{You \textbf{must} use low \vtthreshold (\eg \vtthreshold=4~\cite{miller2016reviewer}) to sample from a distribution of app markets closer to the original population. Using high \vtthreshold will limit the distribution of malware \acp{apk}.}

Nevertheless, dealing with \textit{grayware} remains an open problem that needs addressing. We further discuss this in~\autoref{sec:discussion}.




\subsection{\factorSamplingName}\label{sec:factor-sampling-size}
\subsubsection{Hypothesis}
Ideally, we would evaluate the performance of our Android classifiers against the entire Android population across all of time (or some valid surrogate, such as \androzoo). However, \androzoo alone contains over 25 million samples (at the time of writing) spread across 10+ years, which makes any performance evaluation on the full population infeasible. At the same time, arbitrarily choosing a ``small'' sample size may produce datasets not representative of the underlying population, even if a smaller time frame was selected. 

Many influential papers in Android malware detection, such as~\cite{apigraph_paper,barbero2022transcending,pendlebury2019tesseract,chen2023continuous}, lack a clear rationale on the size of their dataset. Instead, some arbitrarily large sample size is chosen (most likely due to computational limitations) and domain-specific constraints such as Tesseract's Temporal training consistency (C1), Temporal gw/mw windows consistency (C2) and Realistic malware-to-goodware ratio in testing (C3)~\cite{pendlebury2019tesseract} are enforced. On the other hand, Miranda et al.~\cite{miranda2022debiasing} proposed a statistical method for determining the minimum sample size for Android malware datasets, but do not enforce C2 or C3. Rather, opting to rely fully on statistical means to determine a representative dataset. As previously shown in~\autoref{fig:motivational_plot}, even following all prior guidelines and enforcing~\cite{pendlebury2019tesseract} does not completely eliminate spatial and temporal bias. As such, we hypothesize that solely relying on a statistically guided sampling methodology without considering domain knowledge-guided constraints will be insufficient in eliminating spatial-temporal bias. We note that C1 is generally reliant on the Timestamps choice and, as long as proper temporal evaluation is performed, is implicitly applied. 

\hypothesisbox{Hypothesis: \textbf{\factorSamplingName}}{Purely-statistical methods to determine \factorSamplingName under represent the distribution of the Android population and introduce spatial and temporal bias in the dataset.}

\subsubsection{Impact}
We begin exploring a statistically guided sampling methodology for Android malware detection. Based on the traditional \textit{Margin of Error (MoE)} formula~\cite{casella2024statistical} using the finite population correction, Miranda et al.~\cite{miranda2022debiasing} proposed \dada, an approach that computes the smallest sample size for a dataset to be representative of a given population. They use the \textit{Bonferroni correction} to account for general Android characteristics, such as three intervals of \ac{apk} Size, 10 common boolean Android permissions extracted with Droidlysis \cite{apvrille2013pre}, and year of release ranging from 2011 to 2018. However, there is no clear evidence that \ac{apk} size or common boolean Android permissions are important aspects in Android Malware classification. In fact, we noticed that \ac{apk} Size increases across time, questioning if this is an appropriate characteristic for sampling (cf. \autoref{fig:apk_size_over_time} in the Appendix).

\paragraph{Improving \dada}
Instead of applying the \textit{Bonferroni Correction}, we propose focusing on the manual enforcement of the temporal (C2) and spatial (C3) constraints introduced in~\cite{pendlebury2019tesseract}. These two characteristics are not only supported by prior work but are also directly measurable via metadata available from \androzoo and controllable during the sampling process. While the \textit{Bonferroni correction} enhances statistical validity by reducing the risk of false positives, its use in combination with numerous characteristics --- such as \ac{apk} size and a set of common Android permissions --- under the constraints of C2 and C3 would demand an impractically large sample size (see~\autoref{fig:moe_theoretical} in the Appendix). Although larger samples generally yield more representative results, there is a clear trade-off: applying strict statistical corrections to characteristics with limited demonstrated impact on malware dataset evaluation can lead to diminishing returns. In this context, it is more pragmatic to prioritize a smaller number of well-validated factors over attempting to control for many uncertain ones.


We use the same confidence level of $C=99\%$ and margin of error $\delta = 0.015$ as Miranda et al.~\cite{miranda2022debiasing}. We enforce a 9:1 goodware-to-malware ratio such that it satisfies the spatial constraint C3 of Tesseract~\cite{pendlebury2019tesseract}. We then enforce the temporal constraint C2 of Tesseract~\cite{pendlebury2019tesseract} by sampling either monthly/yearly based on only the MoE formula.

We compare the different configurations of MoE against \dada across the same timeframe as \apigraph and \transcend, imitating the configurations both datasets used for sampling. We used \dexdates as they were easily accessible unlike \vtdates. We were unable to use \crawldate despite our recommendation in \autoref{sec:factor-timestamps} as \androzoo was not properly established and crawling was inconsistent in 2015. \autoref{tab:statistical_sampling_1418} shows the total number of samples required across this 5 years and the average number of malware per year. It is evident that due to the relatively small sample size of \dada, the amount of malware is too little for proper evaluation. The same can be said with MoE/Year and MoE/Spatial/Year where the average malware per year is 122 and 61, respectively.

