\subsubsection{Recommendations}
While a larger dataset may better represent a given population, it is often infeasible to obtain a large sample size due to computational and storage constraints. However, by applying statistical sampling algorithms while enforcing constraints for temporal and spatial bias, one can estimate the minimum number of samples required for a dataset that maintains a relatively good representation of the real-world population. As such, one should always enforce temporal and spatial constraints to ensure that the sampled dataset is representative.

\recommendationboxnew{Recommendation: \factorSamplingName}{You \textbf{must} combine statistical methods and available domain knowledge (such as temporal (C2) and spatial (C3) constraints) to determine an appropriate \factorSamplingName for an Android malware dataset.}


\section{Piecing It All Together: A New Dataset} \label{sec:casestudy}

Following from our recommendations in ~\autoref{sec:factor-analysis}, we now show how to apply our guidelines in practice by creating a new dataset and evaluating the performance of the Android malware detectors on it.

\subsection{Composing the Dataset}

As a first step, we sample a dataset considering the five factors introduced in the previous sections.

\paragraph{Time frame selection}
Guided by prior work showing that malware labels tend to stabilize within one year~\cite{miller2016reviewer,zhu2020measuring}, we sample three years of data spanning from 2021 to 2023. This period is recent enough to reflect the current Android malware landscape, while also being long enough to capture the temporal dynamics in Android apps. 


\paragraph{Market selection}
To eliminate the bias caused by sampling from different markets as discussed in \autoref{sec:factor-app-markets}, we solely sample apps from \googleplay for this case study.

\paragraph{Timestamps}
We use publication dates to accurately represent when an app appears in the wild. As discussed \autoref{sec:factor-timestamps}, this is the most reliable timestamp information and in line with the recommendations of the \androzoo authors (see Alecci et al.~\cite{alecci2024androzoo}).


\paragraph{\vtthreshold selection}
In~\autoref{sec:factor-vtt}, we advocate for the use of lower \longvt thresholds. Therefore, we set the \vtthreshold to 4, following the recommendations in Pendlebury et al.~\cite{pendlebury2019tesseract}. This threshold will be used for selecting samples based on the publicly available \androzoo metadata.

\paragraph{Sampling size}
We apply MoE/Spatial/Month, our improved version of \dada~(introduced in~\autoref{sec:factor-sampling-size}), which ensures a statistically representative selection of both malware and goodware samples. In accordance with C3 constraints~\cite{pendlebury2019tesseract}, it enforces a distribution of $10\%$ malware and $90\%$ goodware. The resulting dataset comprises $159,839$ \acp{apk}, including $16,184$ malicious and $143,655$ benign samples. A detailed distribution of the dataset is presented in~\autoref{fig:a2_distribution}.

