\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

While our paper extends the set of best practices for evaluating Android malware classifiers under spatio-temporal drift proposed in prior work~\cite{dos_and_donts,pendlebury2019tesseract,rossow2012prudent,sommer2010outside,flood2024bad}, there are still several open challenges the community faces. In the following, we contextualize our findings by discussing related challenges.

\paragraph{Markets in \androzoo}
\androzoo~\cite{androzoourl} contains primarily \acp{apk} from \googleplay and other western markets, under-representing other regions where Android is the most popular operating system, especially China~\cite{chinese_android}. It has been noted that Chinese app markets are generally less active in protecting mobile users from deceptive or abusive actors; therefore, they tend to have a higher prevalence of malware \cite{wang2018beyond}. At the same time, \androzoo is currently the best repository we have as a community for benign and malicious \acp{apk}.

\paragraph{Grayware}
Our work provides evidence that lower \vtthreshold values better represent the malware population. However, how to account for \textit{grayware}, \ie \acp{apk} detected by at least one \ac{AV} but less than \vtthreshold, which are predominant in \androzoo, remains an open problem. Works such as \cite{andow2016study} have attempted to categorize different types of grayware in \googleplay. However, the taxonomy of grayware remains a problem for Android malware detection.

\paragraph{Single point of failure}
A major unspoken risk comes from the fact that \androzoo and \longvt are single points of failure for the malware community. Alecci et al.~\cite{alecci2024androzoo} highlight that \androzoo in 2024 reached 448TB of data and will continue to grow. This may eventually become unsustainable for a single research team. On the other hand, \longvt puts a cap of 500 queries per user per day, significantly hindering the curation of new datasets. Considering the instability of labels in the wild~\cite{miller2016reviewer}, simply relying on \androzoo is not sufficient. As a community, we should try to reason about what we could do to build more resilient repositories.

\paragraph{Evolution of C3\cite{pendlebury2019tesseract}}
We observed that for \androzoo in 2014--2018, malware is not consistently 10\% across all months. However, obtaining a ``true'' ratio would require help from multiple industrial partners. \cite{pendlebury2019tesseract} estimated the percentage of malware samples at $10\%$ by averaging various sources in 2019, which may no longer be valid in the current landscape. For lack of a better source, using $10\%$ as a spatial constraint will allow consistency in sampled datasets. Future works should investigate the temporal evolution of realistic malware ratios. 

\paragraph{K-Fold evaluation}
Evaluating an ML-based malware detector without temporal constraints would require the use of K-fold cross-validation as shown in~\cite{pendlebury2019tesseract}. While our study is focused on scenarios under temporal drift, the proposed spatial guidelines are still relevant for non-temporal evaluations. This includes \factorTimestampName~\autoref{sec:factor-timestamps}, \factorMarketsName~\autoref{sec:factor-app-markets}, \factorVTTName~\autoref{sec:factor-vtt}, and \factorSamplingName~\autoref{sec:factor-sampling-size}. 


\paragraph{Windows malware}
While our study is focused on Android malware, many of the challenges can be shared with the Windows domain. For example, getting a reliable timestamp source can be difficult, especially when there is no active crawler like \androzoo. Instead, \cite{jiang2024benchmfc} recommends using \textit{first seen} as the best estimate for Windows PE. Another common challenge is \factorTemporalLuckName, since both domains are evaluated in a temporal manner. Thus, our recommendations for avoiding ``Lucky splits'' could be beneficial in the Windows domain.

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_works}

In this section, we cover the research most closely related to our work. We present both seminal research papers tackling the wider problem of achieving fair evaluations in \ac{ML}-based computer security research and more directly comparable publications on dataset sampling and debiasing.

\paragraph{Guidelines for realistic \ac{ML} for computer security} Our research aligns with efforts to establish guidelines for realistic evaluations in security-related applications. Sommer and Paxson~\cite{sommer2010outside} highlighted key challenges in applying \ac{ML} to network intrusion detection systems (NIDS), pointing out fundamental issues that persist in this domain. Rossow et al.~\cite{rossow2012prudent} and Van der Kouwe et al.~\cite{van2019sok} further analyzed common shortcomings and best practices in security system evaluations. However, these works primarily identify challenges and methodological pitfalls without quantifying their impact on classifier performance. Arp et al.~\cite{dos_and_donts} extended this line of work by demonstrating the effects of these pitfalls in real-world scenarios, although their focus remained on general cybersecurity.

In contrast, our work investigates the specific properties and risk factors during dataset creation for the Android malware domain that lead to many of the pitfalls identified in prior studies. 

\paragraph{Temporal evaluations} Other relevant work includes the domain of temporal evaluation primarily raised by Allix et al~\cite{allix2015your} and Miller et al.~\cite{miller2016reviewer}. Recently, Pendlebury et al.~\cite{pendlebury2019tesseract} proposed Tesseract as a framework to measure impact of performance decay. We expand on Tesseract by proposing a new evaluation guideline in \factorTemporalLuckName. Additionally, we address the validity of creation timestamps by experimentally showing the impact of timestamp inconsistencies and providing practical recommendations for researchers to follow (\autoref{sec:factor-timestamps}). 

\paragraph{Realistic datasets for ML-based cybersecurity} Prior work also focused on the problem of realistic datasets and benchmark datasets for ML-based cybersecurity. Sommer and Paxon~\cite{sommer2010outside} highlighted the challenges of creating realistic network intrusion detection datasets, mostly due to privacy concerns; attempts to create datasets artificially, such as DARPA98-99\cite{lippmann20001999} and KDD99\cite{kdd_cup}, failed because they introduced artifacts~\cite{mahoney2003analysis, engelen2021troubleshooting, mchugh2000testing}. Similarly, Jian et al.~\cite{jiang2024benchmfc} created a benchmark dataset for Windows malware by sampling from VirusShare, following a similar dataset curation pipeline to the one we presented. However, we focus on factors related to representativeness, such as sample size and markets, rather than the quality of family labels. \cite{ceschin2024machine} also presented a preliminary discussion on dataset size and markets but lacks empirical evaluation or concrete recommendations, while we provide both. Notably, \cite{ceschin2024machine} calls for better dataset practice---our work directly answers its call. Recently, Flood et al.~\cite{flood2024bad} identified six poor practices in NIDS-specific dataset including poor data diversity, highly dependent features, unclear ground truths, traffic collapse artificial diversity, and wrong labels. Instead of focusing on the limitations of network datasets, we focus our attention on the Android malware domain, where the presence of the \androzoo~\cite{androzoourl,allix2016androzoo,alecci2024androzoo} has offered more opportunities to build more realistic datasets. Furthermore, all our factors are directly applicable during the sampling process, helping to prevent the identified ``bad'' choices in \cite{flood2024bad}. \cite{ge2021impact} analyzed three datasets factors (i.e. class imbalance, quality, and timelines) by examining three state-of-the-art datasets. We differ from this work as we not only show how our sampling factors can affect the sampled distribution but also give actionable recommendations that researchers can follow to reduce the biases highlighted in \cite{ge2021impact}. Additionally, we do not limit our work to only two state-of-the-art datasets, but instead include the wider Android population in our experimental analysis. In conclusion, we identify five factors that can affect realistic evaluations and that can be controlled with actionable recommendations (\autoref{sec:factor-analysis}).

\paragraph{Research on Android malware sampling} Prior work has also attempted to provide algorithms to create representative Android benchmark datasets. Miranda et al. proposed \dada~\cite{miranda2022debiasing}, a two step sampling approach for debiasing Android malware datasets, which combines statistical methods with general \ac{apk} characteristics. The first step is to randomly sample a representative dataset from a population using the margin of classical error to then debias it. However, in~\autoref{sec:factor-sampling-size}, we have shown that \dada lacks important domain-specific knowledge, which is crucial at the sampling stage. In particular, it violates $C3$ of \cite{pendlebury2019tesseract} and P1 of \cite{dos_and_donts}. Recently, Thirumuruganathan et al.\cite{thirumuruganathan2024detecting} proposed an approach to detect and mitigate sampling bias between a labeled training dataset and unlabeled testing dataset based on domain discrimination, whereas our work focuses on the broader picture, questioning how well datasets represent the real-world population, rather than finding a classifier that fits both datasets. 



\section{Conclusion}
We have demonstrated that current best practices~\cite{dos_and_donts,pendlebury2019tesseract,rossow2012prudent,sommer2010outside,flood2024bad} for dataset curation and evaluation of Android malware datasets are insufficient to eliminate spatial and temporal biases. 

To better understand and mitigate this phenomenon, we have identified five factors ---\ie \factorTimestampName, \factorTemporalLuckName, \factorMarketsName, \factorVTTName, and \factorSamplingName---that can directly or indirectly affect the performance evaluation of a learning-based classifier. We have provided actionable recommendations to mitigate their impact through proper sampling strategies and evaluation guidelines.

While the problem of sampling bias remains challenging and vast, this paper provides a first solid milestone towards controllable factors and actionable recommendations, which will allow for more realistic evaluations and fairer comparisons of learning-based systems for malware detection. 

