\paragraph{MoE Theoretical Maximum}
\autoref{sec:factor-sampling-size} presents a statistically guided approach for determining the minimum sample size for Android Malware datasets with the use of domain knowledge. Here we show the cost of naively adding spatial and temporal constraint to original \dada. While it more samples would most likely be more representative of the 'real' population, an impractically high sample size is simply infeasible. In \autoref{fig:moe_theoretical}, we assume a given month has 200,000 samples. Using MoE/Spatial/Month, one would require 7,111 samples per month, 85,332 samples per year. On the other hand, applying both spatial and monthly constraint on \dada would result in a minimum sample size of 21,969 per month, 263,628 per year. 

