\begin{abstract}
% In extreme environments, the swarm of Micro Aerial vehicles (MAVs) can provide valuable information for rescuers. However, localizing and navigating the MAV swarm is a challenge. Existing solutions are not suitable for large-scale MAV swarms in emergency response due to their reliance on expensive equipment and localization infrastructure. To address this, we propose a sensing system based a heterogeneous MAV swarm consisting of a small number of advanced MAVs capable of autonomous localization and a large number of basic MAVs with increasing localization error. The advanced MAVs serve as mobile localization infrastructure for the basic MAVs during sensing. We then introduce \textit{H-Swarm}, a resource allocation system that groups MAV swarm and plans advanced MAVs to allocate sensing resources to limit localization error of basic MAVs. In-field experiments and physical feature-based simulation results show that \textit{H-Swarm} reduces localization error by 64\% on average and improves navigation success rate by 30\% under given constraints compared to baselines.

% Micro Aerial Vehicles (MAV) swarm consists of several resource-intensive but expensive advanced MAVs (AMAVs) and a large group of resource-limited but cost-effective basic MAVs (BMAVs), offering the opportunity to diverse fields. Accurate and real-time localization is a crucial component of MAV swarms and current localization practices lack a low-cost, high-precision, and real-time localization solution for MAV swarms, especially for those lightweight BMAVs. We find an opportunity to accomplish the task by upgrading AMAVs as mobile localization infrastructures for BMAVs. However, turning this insight into a practical system is non-trivial due to the challenges in pose optimization with BMAV’s noisy estimation and resource allocation with AMAV’s dynamic coverage. This study proposes H-Swarm, a new paradigm that transforms AMAVs into mobile localization infrastructures, specifically designed for low-cost and resource-constrained BMAVs. We first design an error-tolerant joint pose estimation model based on an in-depth understanding of the localization error distribution of BMAVs and design a graph-based adaptive grouping-scheduling strategy to allocate resources of AMAVs dynamically. By doing so, H-Swram achieves a dynamic, scalable, and cost-effective localization system suitable for large-scale heterogeneous MAV swarms. We implement H-Swarm on industrial drones and validate its performance. Results show that H-Swarm maintains an average localization error of MAV swarm under $1m$ and a navigation success rate $>95\%$, outperforming the other two baselines by $>60\%$ and $30\%$ respectively. 

A heterogeneous micro aerial vehicles (MAV) swarm consists of resource-intensive but expensive advanced MAVs (AMAVs) and resource-limited but cost-effective basic MAVs (BMAVs), offering opportunities in diverse fields. 
Accurate and real-time localization is crucial for MAV swarms, but current practices lack a low-cost, high-precision, and real-time solution, especially for lightweight BMAVs. 
We find an opportunity to accomplish the task by transforming AMAVs into mobile localization infrastructures for BMAVs. 
However, turning this insight into a practical system is non-trivial due to challenges in location estimation with BMAVs' unknown and diverse localization errors and resource allocation of AMAVs given coupled influential factors. 
This study proposes TransformLoc, a new framework that transforms AMAVs into mobile localization infrastructures, specifically designed for low-cost and resource-constrained BMAVs. 
We first design an error-aware joint location estimation model to perform intermittent joint location estimation for BMAVs and design a similarity-instructed adaptive grouping-scheduling strategy to allocate resources of AMAVs dynamically. 
TransformLoc achieves a collaborative, adaptive, and cost-effective localization system suitable for large-scale heterogeneous MAV swarms. 
We implement TransformLoc on industrial drones and validate performance. 
Results show that TransformLoc outperforms baselines including SOTA up to 68\% in localization performance, motivating up to 60\% navigation success rate improvement.
